window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-anatomy","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants · Practice","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a plant organ shows the following features:\n- An outer layer with unicellular hairs and no cuticle\n- A parenchymatous cortex, a distinct endodermis and a pericycle\n- Eight xylem patches alternating with phloem patches on different radii\n- A large, well-developed pith\nWhich interpretation is correct?","image":"","options":["Dicot root; a cambium ring will later develop between xylem and phloem","Monocot stem; the bundles are conjoint and closed","Monocot root; this organ will not undergo secondary growth","Monocot root; lateral roots will arise from its endodermis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Unicellular hairs with no cuticle indicate a root, not a stem.\nXylem and phloem alternating on different radii is the radial arrangement typical of roots.\nMore than six xylem patches (polyarch) and a large, well-developed pith mark a monocot root. A dicot root has 2–4 xylem patches and a small pith.\nMonocot roots do not undergo secondary growth.\nLateral roots arise from the pericycle, not the endodermis, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Bulliform cells\n(B) Casparian strips\n(C) Pericycle of dicot root\n(D) Endodermis of dicot stem\nList-II\n(i) Starch sheath\n(ii) Initiation of lateral roots\n(iii) Deposition of suberin\n(iv) Inward curling of leaves under water stress","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-i, B-iii, C-ii, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-iv: When bulliform cells in grass leaves become flaccid, the leaf curls inward.\nB-iii: Casparian strips are suberin deposits on the tangential and radial walls of root endodermis.\nC-ii: Lateral roots and the vascular cambium arise from the pericycle.\nD-i: The dicot stem endodermis is rich in starch grains, so it is called the starch sheath.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the transverse section of a young dicot stem, the hypodermis lies just below the epidermis. What type of cells is it made of?","image":"","options":["Sclerenchymatous cells","Collenchymatous cells","Chlorenchyma with large air cavities","Thin-walled parenchyma with large intercellular spaces"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The outermost sub-zone of the dicot stem cortex is the hypodermis.\nIt has a few layers of collenchymatous cells, which give mechanical strength to the young stem.\nA sclerenchymatous hypodermis is a feature of the monocot stem, so option (1) is the tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are included in the stele of a dicot root?\nEpiblema, Cortex, Endodermis, Pericycle, Xylem, Phloem, Conjunctive tissue, Pith","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The stele is made of all tissues on the inner side of the endodermis.\nIncluded: pericycle, xylem, phloem, conjunctive tissue (which lies between xylem and phloem) and pith. That makes 5.\nExcluded: epiblema and cortex, which lie outside the endodermis.\nThe endodermis is also excluded, because it is the innermost layer of the cortex and the stele begins inside it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the transverse section of a sunflower root, what is the outermost layer, many of whose cells protrude as unicellular root hairs, called?","image":"","options":["Endodermis","Epiblema","Pericycle","Hypodermis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The outermost layer of the dicot (sunflower) root is the epiblema.\nMany epiblema cells grow out as unicellular root hairs, which absorb water and minerals.\nThe endodermis and pericycle are inner layers.\nA hypodermis is described for stems, not for the root.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student observes a transverse section of a young root with the following features: unicellular hairs on the outermost layer, a single-layered endodermis with Casparian strips, four xylem patches alternating with four phloem patches, and a very small central pith. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a monocot root, because its vascular bundles are radial.","It is a dicot stem, because it has only a few vascular patches arranged in a ring.","It is a dicot root, and a cambium ring will later develop between its xylem and phloem.","It is a monocot root, which will not undergo secondary growth because its pith is small."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Radial bundles are found in both monocot and dicot roots, so they alone cannot identify the root type.\nThis root has only four xylem patches (dicot roots have 2-4) and a small pith. A monocot root is polyarch (more than six patches) with a large pith.\nIt is therefore a dicot root, in which a cambium ring later develops between xylem and phloem.\nA dicot stem would have conjoint open bundles and multicellular trichomes, not radial patches and root hairs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Cuticle\n(B) Root hair\n(C) Trichome\n(D) Epidermal cell\nList-II\n(i) Unicellular, absorbs water and minerals\n(ii) Large vacuole with thin peripheral cytoplasm\n(iii) Waxy layer that prevents water loss\n(iv) Usually multicellular, may be secretory\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): the cuticle is a waxy layer that prevents water loss.\nB-(i): a root hair is a unicellular elongation that absorbs water and minerals.\nC-(iv): shoot trichomes are usually multicellular and may be secretory.\nD-(ii): an epidermal cell has a large vacuole and a thin lining of cytoplasm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Root hairs, which help a plant absorb water and minerals from the soil, are best described as:","image":"","options":["Multicellular outgrowths of the cortex","Unicellular elongations of epidermal cells","Multicellular, branched epidermal trichomes","Outgrowths arising from pericycle cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes root hairs as unicellular elongations of epidermal (epiblema) cells.\nTheir function is to absorb water and minerals from the soil.\nThe cortex lies below the epidermis, so root hairs do not arise from it.\nMulticellular, branched hairs are trichomes, which are found on the shoot, not the root.\nThe pericycle gives rise to lateral roots, not root hairs, which makes it the most tempting wrong option.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the ground tissue system is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The radially placed parenchyma cells of medullary rays in a primary dicot stem are part of the ground tissue","In an isobilateral leaf, the mesophyll is differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma","The cortex of a dicot stem includes a collenchymatous hypodermis and a starch-rich endodermis","In a dicot stem, the pericycle occurs as semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Medullary rays lie between the vascular bundles and are parenchymatous ground tissue.\n(2) Incorrect: In an isobilateral (monocot) leaf, the mesophyll is NOT differentiated into palisade and spongy tissue. That differentiation is seen in the dorsiventral leaf.\n(3) Correct: The dicot stem cortex has a collenchymatous hypodermis, parenchyma, and an endodermis (the starch sheath).\n(4) Correct: The dicot stem pericycle forms semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the endodermis of a dicot root is correct?","image":"","options":["Its cells are loosely arranged with large intercellular spaces.","Its radial and tangential walls bear Casparian strips of suberin.","Its cells are rich in starch and the layer is called the starch sheath.","Casparian strips are made of water-permeable lignin deposited only on outer walls."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: endodermal cells are barrel-shaped and have no intercellular spaces.\n(2) Correct: the tangential and radial walls have waxy, water-impermeable suberin deposited as Casparian strips.\n(3) Incorrect: the name starch sheath is used by NCERT for the endodermis of the dicot stem.\n(4) Incorrect: the deposit is suberin, it is water-impermeable, and it lies on the radial and tangential walls.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) The tangential and radial walls of endodermal cells of a dicot root bear suberin in the form of casparian strips.\n(ii) Pericycle, vascular bundles and pith together constitute the stele.\n(iii) Parenchyma lying between xylem and phloem in a dicot root is called conjunctive tissue.\n(iv) The cortex of a dicot root is made of compactly arranged cells without intercellular spaces.\n(v) In grasses, turgid bulliform cells make the leaves curl inwards.\nWhich of the above are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (v) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(i), (iii), (iv) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: suberin is deposited as casparian strips on both tangential and radial walls.\n(ii) Correct: all tissues inside the endodermis form the stele.\n(iii) Correct: this is the definition of conjunctive tissue.\n(iv) Incorrect: the root cortex is thin-walled parenchyma with intercellular spaces. It is the endodermis that lacks intercellular spaces.\n(v) Incorrect: curling happens when bulliform cells are flaccid under water stress. Turgid bulliform cells keep the leaf surface exposed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Root hairs are best described as:","image":"","options":["Multicellular branched outgrowths of the cortex","Unicellular elongations of epidermal cells","Multicellular secretory outgrowths of the epidermis","Unicellular outgrowths of the pericycle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: root hairs are unicellular elongations of the epidermal cells of the root.\nThey help absorb water and minerals from the soil.\nThey arise from the epidermis, not from the cortex or the pericycle. The pericycle gives rise to lateral roots instead.\nBeing multicellular or secretory is a feature of shoot trichomes, not root hairs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the endodermis of a sunflower root is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It is the innermost layer of the cortex.","Its cells are barrel-shaped and form a single layer.","Suberin is deposited on its radial and tangential walls as Casparian strips.","Its cells have conspicuous intercellular spaces for gaseous exchange."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the endodermis is the innermost layer of the cortex.\n(2) Correct: it is a single layer of barrel-shaped cells.\n(3) Correct: according to NCERT, the tangential and radial walls carry suberin in the form of Casparian strips.\n(4) Incorrect: endodermal cells have no intercellular spaces. It is the cortical parenchyma outside the endodermis that has intercellular spaces.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are characteristic features of a typical isobilateral (monocot) leaf?\n(i) Stomata present on both the adaxial and abaxial epidermis\n(ii) Mesophyll differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma\n(iii) Vascular bundles of nearly similar size, except in the main veins, as seen in vertical section\n(iv) Veins of varying thickness forming a reticulate network\n(v) In grasses, bulliform cells present in the adaxial epidermis along the veins","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in an isobilateral leaf, stomata occur on both surfaces.\n(ii) Incorrect: the mesophyll is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma. That differentiation is a feature of the dorsiventral (dicot) leaf.\n(iii) Correct: parallel venation gives vascular bundles of near-similar size, except in the main veins.\n(iv) Incorrect: veins of varying thickness in reticulate venation are seen in dicot leaves.\n(v) Correct: bulliform cells are modified adaxial epidermal cells along the veins in grasses.\nSo three features are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a monocot stem, each vascular bundle is surrounded by a:","image":"","options":["Parenchymatous starch sheath","Sclerenchymatous bundle sheath","Collenchymatous bundle sheath","Layer of suberised endodermal cells with casparian strips"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes each scattered vascular bundle of the monocot stem as surrounded by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath.\nThe starch sheath is the endodermis of the dicot stem, not a covering around individual bundles.\nCasparian strips are a feature of the root endodermis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the transverse section of a monocot stem, the hypodermis lying just below the epidermis is made up of which type of cells?","image":"","options":["Collenchyma","Sclerenchyma","Chlorenchyma","Thin-walled parenchyma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the monocot stem has a sclerenchymatous hypodermis.\nCollenchyma is tempting, but a collenchymatous hypodermis belongs to the young dicot stem, where it gives mechanical strength.\nThin-walled parenchyma forms the large ground tissue of the monocot stem, not its hypodermis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Monocotyledonous roots do not show secondary growth.\nReason (R): Unlike in the dicot root, no cambium ring develops between the xylem and phloem in the monocot root.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that monocot roots do not undergo any secondary growth.\nR is true: in the dicot root a cambium ring develops later between the xylem and phloem, but no such cambium forms in the monocot root.\nSecondary xylem and phloem are produced by cambium, so the absence of cambium explains why there is no secondary growth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The vascular bundles of a dicotyledonous stem are called open vascular bundles.\nReason (R): The cambium present between their xylem and phloem gives them the ability to form secondary xylem and secondary phloem.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: dicot stem bundles are conjoint and open.\nR is true: in dicot stems, cambium lies between phloem and xylem and can form secondary xylem and phloem.\nNCERT calls these bundles 'open' precisely because this cambium allows secondary growth, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The hypodermis of a typical young dicot stem is made up of a few layers of:","image":"","options":["Sclerenchymatous cells","Collenchymatous cells","Chlorenchymatous mesophyll cells","Thin-walled parenchyma with water cavities"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a dicot stem, the hypodermis is the outer sub-zone of the cortex, lying just below the epidermis.\nIt consists of a few layers of collenchyma cells, which give mechanical strength to the young stem.\nA sclerenchymatous hypodermis is a feature of the monocot stem, so option (1) is the tempting but wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a transverse section of a sunflower root, a student sees a single layer of barrel-shaped cells with no intercellular spaces. Their tangential and radial walls carry bands of a waxy, water-impermeable material. This layer is the:","image":"","options":["Pericycle","Endodermis","Epiblema","Hypodermis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The innermost layer of the root cortex is the endodermis.\nIt is a single layer of barrel-shaped cells with no intercellular spaces.\nSuberin is deposited on its tangential and radial walls as casparian strips.\nThe pericycle lies just inside the endodermis and has thick-walled parenchyma cells without casparian strips, so it does not fit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a vertical section of a monocot leaf, the vascular bundles appear of nearly similar size, except those in the main veins.\nReason (R): The mesophyll of an isobilateral leaf is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: monocot leaves have parallel venation. This is reflected in vascular bundles of near-similar size, except in the main veins.\nR is true: in an isobilateral leaf, the mesophyll is not divided into palisade and spongy parenchyma.\nHowever, the size of the vascular bundles depends on vein size, which is set by the venation pattern. It does not depend on how the mesophyll is organised.\nSo R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In grasses, the guard cells enclosing the stomatal pore are dumb-bell shaped.\nStatement II: In guard cells, the outer walls (away from the stomatal pore) are highly thickened while the inner walls are thin.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Grasses have dumb-bell shaped guard cells, unlike the usual bean-shaped ones.\nStatement II is false. It reverses the facts. The outer walls, away from the pore, are thin, and the inner walls, towards the pore, are highly thickened.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (tissue of dicot root) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\nA. Epiblema\nB. Endodermis\nC. Pericycle\nD. Cortex\nList-II\nI. Several layers of thin-walled parenchyma with intercellular spaces\nII. Barrel-shaped cells without intercellular spaces\nIII. Bears unicellular root hairs\nIV. Thick-walled parenchyma in which lateral roots originate","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Epiblema: many of its cells protrude as unicellular root hairs (III).\nB. Endodermis: a single layer of barrel-shaped cells with no intercellular spaces (II).\nC. Pericycle: a few layers of thick-walled parenchyma where lateral roots are initiated (IV).\nD. Cortex: several layers of thin-walled parenchyma with intercellular spaces (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"When guard cells become turgid, the stomatal pore opens. Which structural feature of the guard cells best accounts for this?","image":"","options":["The thin outer walls stretch more than the thickened inner walls, so the cells bow outward and the inner walls are pulled apart","The thickened outer walls press inward against the pore and push the inner walls apart","The subsidiary cells absorb water from the guard cells and widen the aperture","The chloroplasts of guard cells release oxygen that inflates the stomatal pore"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT: the outer walls of guard cells (away from the pore) are thin, and the inner walls (towards the pore) are highly thickened.\nOn becoming turgid, the thin outer walls stretch more easily. The guard cells bow outward and drag the thick inner walls away from each other, so the pore opens.\nOption (2) reverses the wall thickening.\nOptions (3) and (4) have no basis in guard cell structure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Bulliform cells\nB. Mesophyll of isobilateral leaf\nC. Abaxial epidermis of dorsiventral leaf\nD. Vascular bundles of monocot leaf\nList-II:\n(i) Not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma\n(ii) Generally bears more stomata than the opposite surface\n(iii) Nearly similar in size, reflecting parallel venation\n(iv) Large, empty, colourless adaxial cells along veins","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-(iv): bulliform cells are large, empty, colourless adaxial epidermal cells along veins.\nB-(i): the mesophyll of an isobilateral leaf is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.\nC-(ii): the abaxial epidermis of a dorsiventral leaf generally bears more stomata.\nD-(iii): monocot leaf bundles are near-similar in size because of parallel venation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A grass plant is well watered in the morning but faces severe water shortage by afternoon. Which option correctly describes its bulliform cells and leaf surface at these two times?","image":"","options":["Morning: bulliform cells turgid, leaf surface exposed; Afternoon: bulliform cells flaccid, leaf curls inwards","Morning: bulliform cells flaccid, leaf curls inwards; Afternoon: bulliform cells turgid, leaf surface exposed","Morning: bulliform cells turgid, leaf curls inwards; Afternoon: bulliform cells flaccid, leaf surface exposed","Bulliform cells stay turgid at both times; only the guard cells change shape"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Morning, with enough water: bulliform cells absorb water and become turgid, so the leaf surface is exposed.\nAfternoon, under water stress: bulliform cells lose water and become flaccid, so the leaf curls inwards and loses less water.\nOption (3) wrongly links turgid bulliform cells with curling.\nOption (4) ignores the role of bulliform cells in leaf rolling.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the dicot root, the tangential and radial walls of endodermal cells carry suberin in the form of casparian strips.\nStatement II: The endodermal cells of the dicot root are loosely arranged with conspicuous intercellular spaces.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Suberin forms casparian strips on both the tangential and radial walls of root endodermal cells.\nStatement II is incorrect. The endodermis is a single layer of barrel-shaped cells with no intercellular spaces.\nIntercellular spaces occur in the cortical parenchyma outside the endodermis, not in the endodermis itself.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines an epidermal peel from a grass leaf under the microscope. Each stoma has two dumb-bell shaped guard cells, flanked by distinct, specially shaped epidermal cells. Which inference is correct?","image":"","options":["The leaf is isobilateral, so stomata are expected on both the adaxial and abaxial epidermis","The guard cell walls facing the pore must be thin, since that is how they surround the pore","Being a monocot, the guard cells must lack chloroplasts","Stomata in such leaves occur only on the abaxial surface, as in dorsiventral leaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Dumb-bell shaped guard cells indicate a grass, which is a monocot with an isobilateral leaf.\nIn isobilateral leaves, stomata are present on both surfaces, so option (1) is correct.\nOption (2) is wrong: the walls of guard cells towards the pore are highly thickened in all stomata.\nOption (3) is wrong: guard cells possess chloroplasts.\nOption (4) is wrong: a dorsiventral leaf has more stomata on its abaxial surface, but that pattern does not apply to an isobilateral grass leaf.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Epidermal cells, being parenchymatous, have dense cytoplasm that fills the whole cell, with only small vacuoles.\nStatement II: In a plant growing in a dry habitat, a thick cuticle and a dense covering of trichomes on the shoot would both help reduce water loss.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: epidermal cells have only a small amount of cytoplasm lining the cell wall and a large vacuole.\nStatement II is correct for two reasons:\n- The waxy cuticle prevents water loss.\n- Trichomes help prevent water loss due to transpiration.\nTogether, these two features suit a dry habitat.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In grasses, the guard cells of the stomata are:","image":"","options":["Bean-shaped","Dumb-bell shaped","Barrel-shaped","Semi-lunar shaped"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says each stoma usually has two bean-shaped guard cells.\nIn grasses, however, the guard cells are dumb-bell shaped.\nBean-shaped is the tempting wrong option. It is the general (dicot-type) form, not the form found in grasses.\nBarrel-shaped describes endodermal cells of the root. Semi-lunar describes the sclerenchymatous pericycle patches of the dicot stem.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Each stoma has two guard cells that enclose a stomatal pore.\n(ii) Subsidiary cells are present around every stoma in all plants.\n(iii) Stomata are structures present in the epidermis of leaves.\n(iv) Guard cells are the only epidermal cells that completely lack plastids.\n(v) The walls of guard cells away from the stomatal pore are thin.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: two guard cells enclose the pore.\n(ii) Incorrect: NCERT says that only sometimes do nearby epidermal cells become specialised as subsidiary cells.\n(iii) Correct: stomata are present in the leaf epidermis.\n(iv) Incorrect: guard cells possess chloroplasts.\n(v) Correct: the outer walls of guard cells are thin.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (region of dicot stem) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(a) Hypodermis\n(b) Endodermis\n(c) Pericycle\n(d) Pith\nList-II\n(i) Semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma\n(ii) Rounded parenchyma with large intercellular spaces in the centre\n(iii) Collenchyma giving mechanical strength\n(iv) Cells rich in starch grains","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Hypodermis is a few layers of collenchyma that strengthen the young stem, so (iii).\n(b) Endodermis is the starch sheath, rich in starch grains, so (iv).\n(c) Pericycle forms semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches above the phloem, so (i).\n(d) Pith is rounded parenchyma with large intercellular spaces in the centre, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a young plant organ shows, from outside inward:\nP. a few layers of collenchyma just below a cuticle-covered epidermis\nQ. a single layer of cells rich in starch grains\nR. semi-lunar sclerenchymatous patches capping the phloem\nS. a central mass of rounded parenchyma with large intercellular spaces\nThe vascular bundles are arranged in a ring. Identify P, Q, R and S respectively.","image":"","options":["Hypodermis, pericycle, endodermis, pith","Cortex, endodermis, bundle sheath, medullary ray","Hypodermis, endodermis, pericycle, pith","Hypodermis, endodermis, pericycle, conjunctive tissue"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A ring of vascular bundles plus a collenchymatous hypodermis indicates a dicot stem.\nP is the hypodermis, the collenchymatous outer zone of the cortex.\nQ is the endodermis, the innermost cortical layer rich in starch and called the starch sheath.\nR is the pericycle, the semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches above the phloem.\nS is the pith, the central rounded parenchyma.\nConjunctive tissue is a feature of roots, so option (4) is wrong. All four regions belong to the ground tissue.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A vertical section of a leaf shows stomata on both surfaces and uniform mesophyll with no palisade layer. It also shows vascular bundles of nearly equal size, apart from the midrib, and groups of large cells in the upper epidermis along the veins. Which inference from these observations is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The leaf most likely has parallel venation","The leaf is likely from a grass, a monocot","The large upper epidermal cells can help the leaf curl under water stress","The large upper epidermal cells are the main photosynthetic cells of the leaf, being rich in chloroplasts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Stomata on both surfaces and undifferentiated mesophyll indicate an isobilateral (monocot) leaf.\nNear-equal bundle sizes reflect parallel venation, so (1) is valid.\nLarge cells in the upper epidermis along veins are bulliform cells, typical of grasses, so (2) is valid.\nBulliform cells cause inward curling when flaccid, so (3) is valid.\nBulliform cells are empty and colourless; photosynthesis is done by the mesophyll, so (4) is incorrect.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The epidermis of roots lacks a cuticle.\nStatement II: Trichomes present on the shoot system are usually unicellular.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The waxy cuticle covers the aerial epidermis to reduce water loss. It is absent in roots, which must absorb water.\nStatement II is incorrect. Trichomes of the shoot are usually multicellular. It is root hairs that are unicellular.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The epidermal hairs present on the stem are known as:","image":"","options":["Root hairs","Trichomes","Bulliform cells","Subsidiary cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"On the stem, the epidermal hairs are called trichomes.\nRoot hairs are found on the root epidermis, not the stem.\nBulliform cells are large, empty cells in the upper epidermis of grass leaves.\nSubsidiary cells are specialised epidermal cells next to the guard cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Conjunctive tissue\n(B) Medullary rays\n(C) Hypodermis of dicot stem\n(D) Pericycle of dicot stem\nList-II\n(i) Semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem\n(ii) Parenchyma lying between xylem and phloem in the root\n(iii) Radially placed parenchyma between vascular bundles\n(iv) Collenchyma layers giving mechanical strength to the young stem","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-ii: Conjunctive tissue is the parenchyma between the xylem and phloem patches of a root.\nB-iii: Medullary rays are radially placed parenchyma cells between the vascular bundles.\nC-iv: The dicot stem hypodermis is collenchymatous and gives strength to the young stem.\nD-i: In the dicot stem, the pericycle forms semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches above the phloem.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Xylem and phloem on alternate radii\n(b) Xylem and phloem on the same radius\n(c) Cambium present in the bundle\n(d) Cambium absent in the bundle\nList-II:\n(i) Closed\n(ii) Radial\n(iii) Open\n(iv) Conjoint","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Xylem and phloem on alternate radii: this is the radial arrangement, as in roots. So a-ii.\n(b) Xylem and phloem jointly on the same radius: this is a conjoint bundle. So b-iv.\n(c) Cambium present: the bundle can form secondary tissues, so it is open. So c-iii.\n(d) Cambium absent: no secondary tissues form, so it is closed. So d-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are characteristic of a typical monocot stem?\n(i) Scattered vascular bundles\n(ii) Sclerenchymatous hypodermis\n(iii) Presence of phloem parenchyma\n(iv) Water-containing cavities within vascular bundles\n(v) Peripheral vascular bundles larger than central ones\n(vi) Open vascular bundles\n(vii) Sclerenchymatous bundle sheath around each vascular bundle","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the bundles are scattered.\n(ii) Correct: the hypodermis is sclerenchymatous.\n(iii) Wrong: phloem parenchyma is absent.\n(iv) Correct: water-containing cavities are present in the bundles.\n(v) Wrong: peripheral bundles are smaller than central ones.\n(vi) Wrong: the bundles are closed.\n(vii) Correct: each bundle has a sclerenchymatous sheath.\nFour features apply.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Cells of this tissue are living and show angular wall thickening. They also provide mechanical support. The tissue is","image":"","options":["Xylem","Sclerenchyma","Collenchyma","Epidermis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Collenchyma cells are living. Their walls are thickened with cellulose, hemicellulose and pectin, mainly at the corners (angular thickening).\nThey give mechanical support to young stems and petioles.\nSclerenchyma is the tempting wrong choice. It also gives support, but its cells are usually dead and have uniform lignified thickening.\nXylem and epidermis do not fit this description.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a dorsiventral leaf are correct?\n(i) A conspicuous cuticle covers both the adaxial and abaxial epidermis.\n(ii) Palisade parenchyma is placed abaxially.\n(iii) Mesophyll is made of parenchyma and carries out photosynthesis.\n(iv) Vascular bundles can be seen in the veins and the midrib.\n(v) Spongy parenchyma cells are compactly arranged without intercellular spaces.\n(vi) The abaxial epidermis may even lack stomata.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: both epidermal surfaces have a conspicuous cuticle.\n(ii) Incorrect: palisade parenchyma is adaxial.\n(iii) Correct: the mesophyll is chloroplast-bearing parenchyma that carries out photosynthesis.\n(iv) Correct: vascular bundles occur in the veins and midrib.\n(v) Incorrect: spongy cells are loose, with large air spaces.\n(vi) Incorrect: it is the adaxial epidermis that may lack stomata.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Casparian strips\nB. Starch sheath\nC. Bulliform cells\nD. Semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma\nList-II\n(i) Pericycle of dicot stem\n(ii) Adaxial epidermis of grass leaf\n(iii) Endodermis of dicot root\n(iv) Endodermis of dicot stem","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Suberin is deposited as casparian strips on the walls of root endodermal cells.\nB-(iv): The dicot stem endodermis is rich in starch grains, so it is called the starch sheath.\nC-(ii): Bulliform cells are modified adaxial epidermal cells of grass leaves.\nD-(i): The dicot stem pericycle occurs as semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches above the phloem.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are seen in a monocot stem?\n(a) Sclerenchymatous hypodermis\n(b) Scattered vascular bundles\n(c) Sclerenchymatous bundle sheath around each bundle\n(d) Phloem parenchyma present\n(e) Water-containing cavities within vascular bundles\n(f) Conjoint and open vascular bundles\n(g) Medullary rays between vascular bundles","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Present: (a) sclerenchymatous hypodermis, (b) scattered bundles, (c) sclerenchymatous bundle sheath, (e) water-containing cavities. That makes 4.\nAbsent:\n(d) Phloem parenchyma is absent in the monocot stem.\n(f) The bundles are conjoint but closed.\n(g) Medullary rays occur between the ring of bundles in a dicot stem, not in a monocot stem.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following together make up the stomatal apparatus?","image":"","options":["Guard cells and stomatal pore only","Stomatal pore, guard cells and subsidiary cells","Guard cells, subsidiary cells and trichomes","Stomatal pore, guard cells and bundle sheath cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines the stomatal apparatus as three parts together: the stomatal aperture, the guard cells and the surrounding subsidiary cells.\nOption (1) leaves out the subsidiary cells.\nTrichomes are epidermal hairs, so option (3) is wrong.\nBundle sheath cells surround vascular bundles, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student observes a transverse section of a plant organ with these features: an outer layer bearing unicellular hairs and lacking a cuticle; a single-layered endodermis followed by pericycle; about fourteen xylem patches alternating with phloem patches on separate radii; a large, well-developed central pith; and no cambium anywhere. The section is most likely of a:","image":"","options":["Dicot root in its primary stage","Monocot stem","Monocot root","Dicot root after secondary growth"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Unicellular hairs, no cuticle and radial bundles indicate a root, which rules out the monocot stem (it has scattered, conjoint bundles).\nAbout fourteen xylem patches means a polyarch condition, which is typical of a monocot root. A dicot root usually has only two to four.\nA large pith and the absence of cambium also point to a monocot root.\nA dicot root after secondary growth would show cambium and secondary tissues.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which option correctly describes a vascular bundle of a typical young dicot stem?","image":"","options":["Conjoint, closed, with endarch protoxylem","Radial, open, with exarch protoxylem","Conjoint, open, with exarch protoxylem","Conjoint, open, with endarch protoxylem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Conjoint: xylem and phloem lie on the same radius, with the phloem on the outer side.\nOpen: cambium is present between the phloem and the xylem.\nEndarch: the protoxylem faces the centre, towards the pith.\nClosed bundles are found in monocots. Radial bundles with exarch xylem are root features.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the palisade parenchyma of a dorsiventral leaf?","image":"","options":["Adaxially placed, elongated cells arranged vertically and parallel to each other","Abaxially placed, oval cells with large air cavities","Loosely arranged round cells that extend to the lower epidermis","Colourless empty cells found along the veins of the upper epidermis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Palisade parenchyma lies on the adaxial side, just below the upper epidermis.\nIts cells are elongated and arranged vertically, parallel to each other.\nOptions (2) and (3) describe spongy parenchyma, which is oval or round, loosely arranged, has air cavities and extends to the lower epidermis.\nOption (4) describes bulliform cells of grasses.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following belong to the epidermal tissue system?\nGuard cells, Root hairs, Trichomes, Subsidiary cells, Endodermis, Mesophyll, Pericycle","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The epidermal tissue system consists of epidermal cells, stomata and epidermal appendages.\nIncluded: guard cells, root hairs, trichomes and subsidiary cells (4).\nExcluded: endodermis, mesophyll and pericycle belong to the ground tissue system.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines a transverse section of a mature plant organ. The xylem and phloem lie in separate patches on alternating radii. There are about eight xylem patches and a large, well-developed pith. The organ is known never to undergo secondary growth. The section is most likely of a:","image":"","options":["Dicot root","Monocot stem","Dicot stem","Monocot root"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Alternating radii means the bundles are radial, which points to a root. This rules out both stems, which have conjoint bundles.\nMore than six xylem patches (polyarch) and a large pith fit a monocot root.\nA dicot root usually has only two to four xylem patches and a small pith. It also later develops a cambium ring.\nThe absence of secondary growth confirms a monocot root.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The vascular bundles of a monocot stem are best described as:","image":"","options":["Radial and closed","Conjoint and open","Conjoint and closed","Radial and open"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Xylem and phloem lie together on the same radius, so the bundles are conjoint. Radial bundles are found in roots.\nNo cambium is present between the xylem and phloem, so the bundles are closed.\nConjoint open bundles, which have cambium, are characteristic of dicot stems.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In an isobilateral (monocot) leaf, where are the stomata found?","image":"","options":["Only on the adaxial (upper) epidermis","On both the adaxial and abaxial epidermis","Only on the abaxial (lower) epidermis","Only along the midrib on the lower surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in an isobilateral leaf, stomata are present on both surfaces of the epidermis.\nIn a dorsiventral (dicot) leaf, the abaxial epidermis generally bears more stomata, and the adaxial epidermis may lack them.\nOption (3) describes a tendency of dicot leaves, not monocot leaves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following belong to the ground tissue system?\nCortex, pericycle, pith, medullary rays, mesophyll, epidermis, stomata, xylem","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says that all tissues except the epidermis and the vascular bundles constitute the ground tissue.\nIncluded: cortex, pericycle, pith, medullary rays and mesophyll (the ground tissue of the leaf). That makes 5.\nExcluded: epidermis and stomata belong to the epidermal tissue system.\nExcluded: xylem belongs to the vascular tissue system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The three tissue systems of flowering plants (epidermal, ground and vascular) are recognised on the basis of","image":"","options":["the number of cell types present in each tissue","the ability of the cells to divide","their structure and location in the plant body","their origin from apical meristems only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that tissue systems are classified on the basis of their structure and location in the plant body.\nThe chapter also notes that the function of a tissue depends on where it lies.\nThe ability to divide is the basis for separating meristematic tissue from permanent tissue, not for defining tissue systems.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The vascular (conducting) tissue system of flowering plants is made up of which tissues?","image":"","options":["Parenchyma and collenchyma, both simple tissues","Xylem and phloem, both complex tissues","Xylem, a simple tissue, and phloem, a complex tissue","Sclerenchyma and phloem, both complex tissues"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: the vascular system consists of complex tissues, the phloem and the xylem. Together they form the vascular bundles.\nParenchyma, collenchyma and sclerenchyma are simple tissues and belong to the ground tissue system.\nXylem is not a simple tissue, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student observes a leaf peel under the microscope. Each stomatal pore is enclosed by two guard cells that are narrow in the middle and swollen at both ends. The peel was most likely taken from a leaf of a:","image":"","options":["Grass","Sunflower","Pea","Mango"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Guard cells that are narrow in the middle and swollen at both ends are dumb-bell shaped.\nIn most plants the guard cells are bean-shaped. In grasses they are dumb-bell shaped.\nSunflower, pea and mango are dicots with bean-shaped guard cells, so the leaf is from a grass.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines a peel from a plant organ under the microscope. The epidermis is covered by a waxy layer, and it bears branched, multicellular hairs, some of which have secretory heads. The peel and the hairs most likely belong to:","image":"","options":["Root epidermis; the hairs are root hairs","Stem epidermis; the hairs are trichomes","Root epidermis; the hairs are trichomes","Stem epidermis; the hairs are root hairs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: A waxy cuticle is present. The cuticle is absent in roots, so this is an aerial organ such as the stem.\nStep 2: The hairs are multicellular, branched and secretory. These features fit shoot trichomes.\nStep 3: Root hairs are unicellular, unbranched and absorptive, so they are ruled out.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the T.S. of a monocot stem are correct?\n(i) Peripheral vascular bundles are generally larger than the centrally located ones.\n(ii) Each vascular bundle is surrounded by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath.\n(iii) Vascular bundles are conjoint and open.\n(iv) Water-containing cavities are present within the vascular bundles.\n(v) A large, conspicuous parenchymatous ground tissue is present.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the peripheral bundles are generally smaller than the central ones.\n(ii) Correct: each scattered bundle has a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath.\n(iii) Incorrect: the bundles are conjoint but closed, because there is no cambium.\n(iv) Correct: water-containing cavities occur within the bundles.\n(v) Correct: the ground tissue is large, conspicuous and parenchymatous.\nThree statements, (ii), (iv) and (v), are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about stomata is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In grasses, the guard cells are dumb-bell shaped","Guard cells contain chloroplasts and regulate the opening and closing of the stomatal pore","Stomatal pore, guard cells and subsidiary cells together form the stomatal apparatus","The walls of guard cells facing the stomatal pore are thin, while the outer walls are highly thickened"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: grass guard cells are dumb-bell shaped; others are bean-shaped.\n(2) Correct: guard cells possess chloroplasts and control stomatal opening.\n(3) Correct: this is the definition of the stomatal apparatus.\n(4) Incorrect: the walls are the reverse. The inner walls (towards the pore) are highly thickened, and the outer walls are thin.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In vertical sections of monocot leaves, the vascular bundles are of nearly similar size, except in the main veins.\nStatement II: In dorsiventral leaves, the adaxial epidermis generally bears more stomata than the abaxial epidermis.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: parallel venation in monocot leaves shows up as near-similar sizes of vascular bundles, except in the main veins.\nStatement II is incorrect: it is the abaxial (lower) epidermis that generally bears more stomata, and the adaxial epidermis may even lack them.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines the T.S. of a young stem and notes the following: a collenchymatous hypodermis, an endodermis rich in starch grains, semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches above the phloem, and vascular bundles arranged in a ring. Which additional observation would contradict her identification of the section as a dicot stem?","image":"","options":["Radially placed parenchyma cells between the bundles","Protoxylem lying towards the centre of the stem","Water-containing cavities within the vascular bundles","Cambium between the xylem and phloem of each bundle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Medullary rays, endarch protoxylem and intrafascicular cambium are all normal features of a dicot stem.\nWater-containing cavities inside the vascular bundles are a monocot stem feature, often found together with absent phloem parenchyma.\nOnly option (3) is inconsistent with a dicot stem.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature of the mesophyll distinguishes an isobilateral leaf from a dorsiventral leaf?","image":"","options":["Mesophyll is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma","Mesophyll consists only of palisade parenchyma","Mesophyll is made of sclerenchyma instead of parenchyma","Mesophyll lacks chloroplasts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a dorsiventral leaf, the mesophyll has adaxial palisade parenchyma and spongy parenchyma below it.\nIn an isobilateral leaf, the mesophyll is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.\nThe mesophyll is still photosynthetic parenchyma with chloroplasts, so options (3) and (4) are wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Xylem and phloem are complex tissues.\n(ii) In conjoint vascular bundles, phloem is usually located only on the outer side of xylem.\n(iii) Conjoint vascular bundles are characteristic of roots.\n(iv) The vascular tissue in a monocot root is arranged radially.\n(v) Closed vascular bundles can produce secondary xylem.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT describes xylem and phloem as complex tissues.\n(ii) Correct: conjoint bundles usually have phloem only on the outer side of xylem.\n(iii) Incorrect: conjoint bundles are common in stems and leaves. Roots have radial bundles.\n(iv) Correct: all roots, including monocot roots, have a radial arrangement.\n(v) Incorrect: closed bundles lack cambium, so they cannot form secondary tissues.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a dorsiventral leaf, the abaxial epidermis generally bears more stomata than the adaxial epidermis.\nStatement II: In an isobilateral leaf, the mesophyll is differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the lower (abaxial) epidermis of a dicot leaf usually has more stomata. The upper (adaxial) epidermis may even lack them.\nStatement II is incorrect: the isobilateral (monocot) leaf has undifferentiated mesophyll. The palisade and spongy layers are a feature of the dorsiventral leaf.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a transverse section of a young monocot stem, the layer just below the epidermis (hypodermis) is made up of which tissue?","image":"","options":["Collenchyma","Sclerenchyma","Chlorenchyma","Thin-walled parenchyma with large intercellular spaces"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the monocot stem has a sclerenchymatous hypodermis.\nCollenchyma is the tempting option, but a collenchymatous hypodermis that gives mechanical strength to the young stem is a feature of the dicot stem.\nRounded, thin-walled parenchyma with conspicuous intercellular spaces describes the cortical layers below the hypodermis in a dicot stem. It is not the hypodermis.\nSo the monocot hypodermis is sclerenchymatous.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features would be observed in the T.S. of a typical monocot stem?\n(i) Sclerenchymatous hypodermis\n(ii) Cambium between xylem and phloem\n(iii) Water-containing cavities within vascular bundles\n(iv) Phloem parenchyma\n(v) Scattered vascular bundles, each with a sclerenchymatous sheath\n(vi) Peripheral vascular bundles larger than central ones\n(vii) Semi-lunar patches of sclerenchymatous pericycle above the phloem","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Present.\n(ii) Absent: the bundles are closed.\n(iii) Present.\n(iv) Absent: phloem parenchyma is missing in the monocot stem.\n(v) Present.\n(vi) Absent: the peripheral bundles are smaller than the central ones.\n(vii) Absent: semi-lunar patches of sclerenchymatous pericycle are a dicot stem feature.\nPresent features are (i), (iii) and (v), which makes 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Epidermal hairs on the stem help in preventing water loss due to transpiration.\nReason (R): The trichomes of the shoot system are usually unicellular and always unbranched.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that trichomes help in preventing water loss due to transpiration.\nR is false: shoot trichomes are usually multicellular, not unicellular.\nThey may also be branched or unbranched, so 'always unbranched' is wrong too.\nThe unicellular epidermal hairs are the root hairs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a vascular bundle of the midrib in a dorsiventral leaf (Figure 6.5a of NCERT), the xylem is positioned:","image":"","options":["Towards the adaxial (upper) side, with phloem towards the abaxial (lower) side","Towards the abaxial (lower) side, with phloem towards the adaxial (upper) side","In the centre, completely surrounded by phloem","Alternating with phloem on different radii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Leaf vascular bundles are conjoint, so xylem and phloem lie on the same radius.\nIn the dicot leaf section, xylem faces the adaxial (upper) surface and phloem faces the abaxial (lower) surface.\nThis follows the stem pattern, where phloem lies on the outer side of xylem; in the leaf, the stem's outer side corresponds to the lower surface.\nAlternating xylem and phloem on different radii is the radial arrangement of roots.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The hypodermis of a young dicot stem gives it mechanical strength. Which simple tissue is it made of?","image":"","options":["Sclerenchyma","Parenchyma with large air cavities","Collenchyma","Chlorenchymatous mesophyll"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The hypodermis is the outer sub-zone of the cortex in a dicot stem.\nIt consists of a few layers of collenchymatous cells lying just below the epidermis, and these give mechanical strength to the young stem.\nThe tempting option, sclerenchyma, forms the hypodermis of the monocot stem, not the dicot stem.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a dicot root, lateral roots are initiated in which tissue?","image":"","options":["Endodermis","Cortex","Pericycle","Pith"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pericycle is a few layers of thick-walled parenchymatous cells lying just inside the endodermis.\nLateral roots are initiated in the pericycle. During secondary growth, initiation of the vascular cambium also takes place in these cells.\nThe endodermis lies next to the pericycle but does not give rise to lateral roots.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a dicot stem, the pericycle is present:","image":"","options":["As a continuous ring of thin-walled parenchyma below the phloem","As semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem, inner to the endodermis","As a single layer of barrel-shaped cells with casparian strips","As radially placed parenchyma cells between vascular bundles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT places the dicot stem pericycle on the inner side of the endodermis and above the phloem.\nIt occurs as semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma.\nOption (3) describes the endodermis of a root.\nOption (4) describes the medullary rays.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a monocot stem are correct?\n(i) Vascular bundles are scattered in the ground tissue.\n(ii) Each vascular bundle is surrounded by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath.\n(iii) Vascular bundles are conjoint and open.\n(iv) Peripheral vascular bundles are generally larger than the central ones.\n(v) Phloem parenchyma is absent.\n(vi) Water-containing cavities are present within the vascular bundles.","image":"","options":["Three","Five","Two","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the bundles are scattered.\n(ii) Correct: each bundle has a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath.\n(iii) Incorrect: the bundles are closed, because there is no cambium.\n(iv) Incorrect: the peripheral bundles are smaller than the central ones.\n(v) Correct: phloem parenchyma is absent.\n(vi) Correct: water-containing cavities are present in the bundles.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the T.S. of a monocot stem, the vascular bundles are arranged in a ring, with medullary rays between them.\nStatement II: The monocot stem has a large, conspicuous ground tissue made of parenchyma.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A ring of bundles with medullary rays between them is characteristic of the dicot stem. In the monocot stem, the bundles are scattered.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT describes a large, conspicuous parenchymatous ground tissue in the monocot stem, and the scattered bundles lie within it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A vertical section of a leaf shows:\n- stomata on both the upper and lower epidermis\n- mesophyll not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma\n- vascular bundles of nearly similar size, except in the main veins\n\nWhich inference is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a dorsiventral dicot leaf with reticulate venation","It is an isobilateral leaf with parallel venation. If it is a grass, its bulliform cells lie in the adaxial epidermis and curl the leaf inwards when they become flaccid","It is an isobilateral leaf. Its bulliform cells lie in the abaxial epidermis and expose the leaf surface when they become flaccid","It is a dorsiventral leaf in which spongy parenchyma has replaced the palisade layer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Stomata on both surfaces and undifferentiated mesophyll indicate an isobilateral (monocot) leaf.\nBundles of nearly similar size reflect parallel venation. Reticulate venation gives veins of varying thickness, which rules out option (1).\nIn grasses, bulliform cells are adaxial epidermal cells along the veins. When they are flaccid, the leaf curls inwards. When they are turgid, the leaf surface is exposed.\nOption (3) reverses both the location and the effect of bulliform cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the ground tissue system of a plant includes:","image":"","options":["Only the cortex and the pith","All tissues except the epidermis and the vascular bundles","All tissues except the vascular bundles","Only the parenchyma cells of the plant body"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines ground tissue by exclusion: every tissue except the epidermis and the vascular bundles.\nThe cortex and pith are only part of it. The pericycle, medullary rays and leaf mesophyll also belong to it.\nOption (3) is wrong because it would wrongly place the epidermis in the ground tissue.\nOption (4) is wrong because ground tissue also has collenchyma and sclerenchyma.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In an isobilateral leaf, the mesophyll is differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.\nStatement II: In vertical sections of monocot leaves, vascular bundles (except in main veins) are of nearly similar size, reflecting parallel venation.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The mesophyll of an isobilateral (monocot) leaf is not differentiated. Palisade and spongy layers are a feature of the dorsiventral (dicot) leaf.\nStatement II is correct. Parallel venation gives near-similar bundle sizes. In contrast, the reticulate venation of dicots gives veins of varying thickness.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cuticle, the waxy layer that reduces water loss, is absent in roots.\nStatement II: Root hairs are unicellular outgrowths of epidermal cells that help absorb water and minerals.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Roots absorb water, so they have no cuticle. In aerial parts, the cuticle prevents water loss.\nStatement II is correct. Root hairs are unicellular elongations of epidermal cells and absorb water and minerals from the soil.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Phellogen and Phellem respectively denote","image":"","options":["Cork and cork cambium","Cork cambium and cork","Secondary cortex and cork","Cork and secondary cortex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Phellogen is the cork cambium. It is a meristematic layer that develops in the cortex during secondary growth.\nPhellem is the cork. Phellogen cuts it off towards the outside, and its cells become suberised and dead.\nOn the inner side, phellogen produces phelloderm, the secondary cortex.\nSo the correct order is cork cambium, then cork. Option (1) reverses this order. Options (3) and (4) confuse phellem with phelloderm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Radial arrangement\n(b) Conjoint open bundle\n(c) Conjoint closed bundle\n(d) Complex tissue of vascular system\nList-II\n(i) Monocot stem\n(ii) Sunflower root\n(iii) Phloem\n(iv) Dicot stem\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): Roots, such as the sunflower root, have xylem and phloem on alternate radii, which is the radial arrangement.\n(b)-(iv): Dicot stem bundles are conjoint and contain cambium, so they are open.\n(c)-(i): Monocot stem bundles are conjoint but have no cambium, so they are closed.\n(d)-(iii): The vascular system consists of the complex tissues xylem and phloem.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure in a dorsiventral leaf) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\nA. Adaxial epidermis\nB. Spongy parenchyma\nC. Bundle sheath\nD. Cuticle\nList-II:\n(i) Layer of thick-walled cells surrounding a vascular bundle\n(ii) Conspicuous waxy covering that reduces water loss\n(iii) Upper surface layer that may lack stomata\n(iv) Loosely arranged oval or round cells with large air cavities\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): The adaxial epidermis is the upper surface and may have no stomata.\nB-(iv): Spongy parenchyma has oval or round, loosely arranged cells with large spaces and air cavities between them.\nC-(i): A layer of thick-walled bundle sheath cells surrounds each vascular bundle.\nD-(ii): The leaf epidermis has a conspicuous waxy cuticle that prevents water loss.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In leaves, the ground tissue made of thin-walled cells containing chloroplasts is called:","image":"","options":["Pith","Cortex","Mesophyll","Bundle sheath"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In leaves, the ground tissue lies between the upper and lower epidermis.\nIt is made of thin-walled cells that contain chloroplasts, and it is called mesophyll.\nCortex and pith are ground-tissue zones of stems and roots, not of the leaf lamina.\nThe bundle sheath is the layer of thick-walled cells around the vascular bundles.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The stomatal apparatus includes:","image":"","options":["Stomatal pore and guard cells only","Guard cells and subsidiary cells only","Stomatal aperture, guard cells and the surrounding subsidiary cells","Guard cells, subsidiary cells and trichomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines the stomatal apparatus as three parts taken together: the stomatal aperture, the guard cells and the surrounding subsidiary cells.\nOption (1) leaves out the subsidiary cells.\nOption (2) leaves out the pore.\nOption (4) adds trichomes. Trichomes are epidermal appendages, not part of the stoma.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the vertical section of a dicot leaf, the vascular bundles are of noticeably different sizes.\nReason (R): In reticulate venation, the veins vary in thickness, and the size of a vascular bundle depends on the size of its vein.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the vascular bundles in a dicot leaf section are of unequal sizes.\nR is true: according to NCERT, the size of a vascular bundle depends on the size of its vein, and veins vary in thickness in the reticulate venation of dicot leaves.\nR therefore directly explains A.\nContrast this with monocot leaves. Their parallel venation gives bundles of nearly similar size, except in the main veins.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the pith of a monocot root?","image":"","options":["Small or inconspicuous","Large and well developed","Absent; the centre is filled with metaxylem","Replaced by a cambium ring"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the pith of a monocot root as large and well developed.\n'Small or inconspicuous' is the tempting choice, but it describes the pith of a dicot root.\nThe pith is not absent, and no cambium forms in a monocot root.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Bulliform cells of grass leaves are modified forms of which cells?","image":"","options":["Spongy parenchyma cells near the lower epidermis","Bundle sheath cells around the veins","Adaxial epidermal cells along the veins","Guard cells of the abaxial epidermis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that in grasses, certain adaxial (upper) epidermal cells along the veins modify into bulliform cells.\nThey are therefore epidermal in origin, not mesophyll or bundle sheath cells.\nThey are on the upper surface, so option (4), which places them in the abaxial epidermis, is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following belong to the ground tissue system?\nCortex, pericycle, pith, medullary rays, epiblema, root hairs, xylem, mesophyll, endodermis","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ground tissue includes everything except the epidermis and the vascular bundles.\nIncluded: cortex, pericycle, pith, medullary rays and mesophyll.\nThe endodermis is also included, because it is the innermost layer of the cortex.\nExcluded: epiblema and root hairs, which are epidermal, and xylem, which is vascular.\nThat gives 6 ground-tissue components.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Guard cells possess chloroplasts and regulate the opening and closing of stomata.\nReason (R): Ordinary epidermal cells are parenchymatous, with a large vacuole and a small amount of cytoplasm lining the cell wall.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that guard cells possess chloroplasts and regulate the opening and closing of stomata.\nR is also true. Epidermal cells are parenchymatous, with a large vacuole and thin peripheral cytoplasm.\nHowever, R only describes ordinary epidermal cells. It says nothing about why guard cells have chloroplasts or control the pore.\nSo R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a young dicot stem, the collenchymatous hypodermis just below the epidermis provides mechanical strength.\nStatement II: In each vascular bundle of a dicot stem, the protoxylem lies towards the periphery and the metaxylem lies towards the pith.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that the few layers of collenchyma in the hypodermis give mechanical strength to the young stem.\nStatement II is incorrect: dicot stem bundles have endarch protoxylem. This means the protoxylem faces the centre (pith) and the metaxylem lies towards the periphery.\nThe arrangement described in Statement II, with protoxylem towards the periphery, would be exarch.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a transverse section of a monocot root, the number of xylem bundles is usually more than six. What is this condition called?","image":"","options":["Diarch","Tetrarch","Hexarch","Polyarch"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that a monocot root usually has more than six xylem bundles. This is the polyarch condition.\nDiarch (two) and tetrarch (four) describe the fewer bundles seen in dicot roots, which usually have two to four xylem patches.\nHexarch means exactly six bundles, so it does not fit 'more than six'.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Inner wall of guard cell\n(B) Outer wall of guard cell\n(C) Subsidiary cells\n(D) Stomatal pore\nList-II\n(i) Thin wall, facing away from the pore\n(ii) Highly thickened wall\n(iii) Opening enclosed by the two guard cells\n(iv) Epidermal cells near the guard cells, specialised in shape and size\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The inner wall faces the pore and is highly thickened, so A-(ii).\n(B) The outer wall faces away from the pore and is thin, so B-(i).\n(C) Subsidiary cells are epidermal cells near the guard cells that are specialised in shape and size, so C-(iv).\n(D) The stomatal pore is the opening enclosed by the two guard cells, so D-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the epidermis are correct?\n(i) It is usually single-layered.\n(ii) Its cells are loosely arranged with large intercellular spaces.\n(iii) It is the outermost layer of the primary plant body.\n(iv) Its cells are elongated and form a continuous layer.\n(v) The root epidermis is covered by a thick cuticle.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the epidermis is usually single-layered.\n(ii) Incorrect: epidermal cells are compactly arranged and form a continuous layer.\n(iii) Correct: it is the outermost layer of the primary plant body.\n(iv) Correct: the cells are elongated and form a continuous layer.\n(v) Incorrect: the cuticle is absent in roots.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the primary dicot root, what are the parenchymatous cells lying between the xylem and phloem patches called?","image":"","options":["Medullary rays","Conjunctive tissue","Bundle sheath","Pith"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the dicot root, xylem and phloem form separate patches.\nThe parenchyma cells that fill the space between these patches are called conjunctive tissue.\nMedullary rays are the radially placed parenchyma between vascular bundles in the dicot stem, so option (1) is wrong.\nThe pith lies in the centre and is small in this root.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines a vertical section of a leaf. It shows a thick cuticle, an upper epidermis without stomata, and vascular bundles of varying size, each enclosed by thick-walled bundle sheath cells. She concludes it is a dorsiventral (dicot) leaf. Which additional observation would contradict her conclusion?","image":"","options":["Elongated, vertically arranged cells lie just below the upper epidermis","Many stomata are present in the lower epidermis","The mesophyll is uniform, with no distinction into palisade and spongy parenchyma","Loosely arranged cells with large air cavities lie just above the lower epidermis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Options (1) and (4) describe palisade and spongy parenchyma. Both are expected in a dorsiventral leaf.\nOption (2), many stomata on the abaxial (lower) surface, is also typical of a dorsiventral leaf.\nOption (3), an undifferentiated mesophyll, is characteristic of an isobilateral (monocot) leaf. It is the only observation that conflicts with her identification.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The root of sunflower can undergo secondary growth.\nReason (R): The primary vascular bundles of a dicot root are of the conjoint open type, with cambium present between xylem and phloem.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. A cambium ring develops between xylem and phloem later, and the pericycle initiates vascular cambium during secondary growth. So the sunflower root shows secondary growth.\nR is false. In the primary root, xylem and phloem lie as alternate patches on different radii, which is the radial arrangement, not conjoint.\nThe primary root bundles contain no cambium. The cambium ring forms only later.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Subsidiary cells are best described as:","image":"","options":["Epidermal cells near the guard cells that have become specialised in shape and size","Mesophyll cells lying just beneath the stomatal pore","Thick-walled bundle sheath cells surrounding the veins","Large, empty, colourless cells along the veins of grass leaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT: a few epidermal cells in the vicinity of the guard cells sometimes become specialised in shape and size. These are called subsidiary cells.\nSo they are epidermal in origin, not mesophyll (option 2) or bundle sheath (option 3) cells.\nOption (4) describes bulliform cells of grasses, which are a different modification.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During water stress, leaves of grasses curl inwards.\nReason (R): Bulliform cells become flaccid when they lose water.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: grass leaves curl inwards under water stress, which minimises water loss.\nR is true: under water stress, bulliform cells lose water and become flaccid.\nThe flaccid bulliform cells are what make the leaf curl inwards, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are included in the stele of a dicot root?\nEpiblema, cortex, endodermis, pericycle, vascular bundles, pith","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The stele is made of all the tissues inside the endodermis.\nIncluded: pericycle, vascular bundles and pith, which makes 3.\nNot included: epiblema and cortex lie outside the endodermis.\nThe endodermis itself is the innermost cortical layer, so it is also outside the stele. This is the most tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the T.S. of a young dicot stem are correct?\n(i) The epidermis is covered with a thin layer of cuticle.\n(ii) The epidermis bears numerous stomata, more than a leaf.\n(iii) Cortical cells below the hypodermis are rounded, thin-walled parenchyma with conspicuous intercellular spaces.\n(iv) Vascular bundles are scattered throughout the ground tissue.\n(v) The protoxylem of each vascular bundle is endarch.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the epidermis has a thin cuticle.\n(ii) Incorrect: the epidermis may bear only a few stomata.\n(iii) Correct: this matches the NCERT description of the cortical parenchyma.\n(iv) Incorrect: the bundles are arranged in a ring. Scattered bundles are a feature of the monocot stem.\n(v) Correct: the bundles have endarch protoxylem.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines transverse sections of four plant organs:\nP – xylem and phloem on alternate radii, with more than six xylem patches and a large pith\nQ – conjoint bundles arranged in a ring, with cambium between xylem and phloem\nR – conjoint bundles scattered in ground tissue, each with a sclerenchymatous sheath and no cambium\nS – xylem and phloem on alternate radii, with two to four xylem patches and a small pith\nWhich of these organs can show secondary growth?","image":"","options":["Q only","Q and S","Q and R","P, Q and S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"P has a polyarch radial arrangement and a large pith, so it is a monocot root. Monocot roots show no secondary growth.\nQ has open conjoint bundles in a ring, so it is a dicot stem. Its cambium forms secondary tissues.\nR has closed, scattered bundles, so it is a monocot stem. It cannot form secondary tissues.\nS has 2–4 xylem patches and a small pith, so it is a dicot root. Later, a cambium ring develops between its xylem and phloem, allowing secondary growth.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following components is absent from the vascular bundles of a monocot stem?","image":"","options":["Xylem vessels","Sieve tubes","Phloem parenchyma","Companion cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT specifically notes that phloem parenchyma is absent in the vascular bundles of the monocot stem.\nThe phloem still conducts food, so sieve tubes and companion cells are present.\nXylem is present because the bundles are conjoint, with both xylem and phloem.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the vertical section of a dorsiventral leaf, which tissue lies on the adaxial side and is made of elongated cells arranged vertically and parallel to one another?","image":"","options":["Spongy parenchyma","Palisade parenchyma","Bundle sheath","Bulliform cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The mesophyll of a dorsiventral leaf has two kinds of parenchyma.\nPalisade parenchyma lies on the adaxial (upper) side. Its cells are elongated and stand vertically, parallel to each other.\nSpongy parenchyma lies below the palisade. Its cells are oval or round and loosely arranged, so option (1) is wrong.\nBundle sheath cells surround the vascular bundles. Bulliform cells occur in grass leaves, not in dicot leaves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about leaf anatomy are correct?\n(i) In an isobilateral leaf, the mesophyll is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.\n(ii) Bulliform cells are modified abaxial epidermal cells.\n(iii) When bulliform cells are turgid, the leaf curls inward.\n(iv) In a dorsiventral leaf, the vascular bundles are surrounded by thick-walled bundle sheath cells.\n(v) Spongy parenchyma is loosely arranged with air cavities and extends to the lower epidermis.\n(vi) Parallel venation in monocot leaves is reflected in the near similar sizes of vascular bundles, except in main veins.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the mesophyll of an isobilateral leaf is undifferentiated.\n(ii) Incorrect: bulliform cells are adaxial epidermal cells along the veins.\n(iii) Incorrect: turgid bulliform cells keep the leaf surface exposed. The leaf curls inward when they become flaccid.\n(iv) Correct: the bundles are surrounded by a thick-walled bundle sheath.\n(v) Correct: spongy parenchyma is loosely arranged with air cavities and extends to the lower epidermis.\n(vi) Correct: parallel venation gives bundles of near similar size.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Stomata regulate the processes of transpiration and gaseous exchange.\nReason (R): Guard cells, unlike chloroplast-bearing mesophyll cells, lack chloroplasts.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that stomata regulate transpiration and gaseous exchange.\nR is false. Guard cells possess chloroplasts and regulate the opening and closing of stomata. Ordinary epidermal cells mostly lack chloroplasts, but guard cells have them.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section shows the following features. There is an outer epidermis bearing unicellular hairs, a parenchymatous cortex, and an endodermis with casparian strips. Inside these are about eight xylem patches alternating with phloem patches on different radii, and a large, well-developed central pith. Which identification and prediction is correct?","image":"","options":["Dicot root; a cambium ring will later develop between xylem and phloem","Monocot stem; its vascular bundles are conjoint and closed","Dicot stem; its vascular bundles are conjoint and open","Monocot root; it will not undergo secondary growth"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Unicellular hairs on the epidermis and casparian strips in the endodermis indicate a root.\nXylem and phloem alternating on different radii is the radial arrangement found in roots, which rules out both stems.\nMore than six xylem patches (polyarch) and a large pith point to a monocot root. A dicot root has 2–4 patches and a small pith.\nMonocot roots show no secondary growth.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student observes a transverse section with the following features:\n- an outer layer bearing unicellular hairs\n- a wide parenchymatous cortex whose innermost layer shows Casparian strips\n- a few layers of thick-walled parenchyma inside that layer\n- four xylem patches alternating with four phloem patches on different radii\n- a very small pith\nWhich conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a monocot root, because the xylem and phloem lie on alternate radii.","It is a dicot stem, because the vascular bundles are conjoint and open.","It is a dicot root with radial vascular bundles, in which a cambium ring can later develop between xylem and phloem.","It is a dicot root that cannot undergo secondary growth, because its bundles lack cambium."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Unicellular hairs, Casparian strips, alternate xylem and phloem, 2–4 patches and a small pith together indicate a dicot root.\nXylem and phloem on alternate radii form a radial arrangement.\nLater, a cambium ring develops between the xylem and phloem.\n(1) fails: a monocot root is polyarch (more than six xylem bundles) and has a large pith.\n(2) fails: alternate radii means the bundles are radial, not conjoint.\n(4) fails: dicot roots do undergo secondary growth.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the vascular bundles of a dicot stem, the protoxylem lies towards the pith and the phloem lies on the outer side of the xylem.\nStatement II: The cortex of a dicot stem extends from just below the epidermis up to the pith and includes the pericycle as its innermost layer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the bundles are endarch, so the protoxylem faces the centre, and being conjoint they have the phloem on the outer side.\nStatement II is incorrect: the cortex lies between the epidermis and the pericycle.\nThe cortex has three sub-zones: hypodermis, general cortex and endodermis. Its innermost layer is the endodermis, not the pericycle.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"What is the fate of primary xylem in a dicot root showing extensive secondary growth?","image":"","options":["It is retained in the centre of the axis","It gets crushed","May or may not get crushed","It gets surrounded by primary phloem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a dicot root, the vascular cambium first forms inside the phloem bundles and then becomes a wavy ring.\nThis cambium adds secondary xylem towards the inside, around the primary xylem, and secondary phloem towards the outside.\nThe primary xylem therefore remains intact in the centre of the axis.\nIt is the primary phloem, pushed outward, that gets crushed. That is why option (2) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q18)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the T.S. of a monocot stem is correct?","image":"","options":["Vascular bundles near the periphery are generally larger than those in the centre","Vascular bundles near the periphery are generally smaller than those in the centre","All vascular bundles are of the same size and form a ring","Vascular bundles are restricted to the central pith region"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: this reverses the actual size relationship.\n(2) Correct: NCERT states that peripheral bundles are generally smaller than the centrally located ones.\n(3) Incorrect: a ring of bundles is characteristic of the dicot stem, and the bundles also vary in size.\n(4) Incorrect: the bundles are scattered throughout the ground tissue, and the monocot stem has no distinct pith.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following pairs of parts of a flowering plant is epidermis absent?","image":"","options":["Root tip and shoot tip","Shoot bud and floral bud","Ovule and seed","Petiole and pedicel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Epidermis is a differentiated protective layer formed from protoderm.\nAt the root tip and shoot tip, the apical meristems are still actively dividing. The root tip is covered by the root cap, and the shoot apex has only undifferentiated protoderm, so no mature epidermis exists there.\nShoot buds, floral buds, petioles and pedicels all have epidermis. Ovules and seeds also have outer protective layers (the integuments and the seed coat).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three tissue systems found in flowering plants?","image":"","options":["Epidermal tissue system","Ground or fundamental tissue system","Vascular or conducting tissue system","Meristematic tissue system"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists three tissue systems: epidermal, ground (fundamental) and vascular (conducting).\nMeristematic tissue is a category of tissues based on the capacity of their cells to divide. It is classified as apical, lateral or intercalary.\nIt is not a tissue system defined by location.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the transverse section of a monocot root, xylem and phloem bundles lie alternately on different radii.\nStatement II: The pith in a monocot root is small or inconspicuous.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: roots have radial vascular bundles, with xylem and phloem alternating along different radii. This holds for the monocot root as well.\nStatement II is incorrect: the pith of a monocot root is large and well developed.\nA small or inconspicuous pith is a feature of the dicot root.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many xylem patches are usually present in the primary dicot root, such as sunflower?","image":"","options":["Only one","Two to four","Six to eight","More than six (polyarch)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the dicot root usually has two to four xylem and phloem patches.\nHaving more than six xylem bundles (polyarch) is a feature of the monocot root.\nSo the dicot root has fewer xylem bundles than the monocot root.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical dorsiventral (dicot) leaf, which surface generally bears more stomata?","image":"","options":["Adaxial (upper) epidermis","Both surfaces bear exactly equal numbers","Abaxial (lower) epidermis","Neither surface; stomata occur only on the midrib"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In a dorsiventral leaf, the abaxial (lower) epidermis generally bears more stomata than the adaxial (upper) epidermis.\nThe adaxial epidermis may even lack stomata altogether.\nA roughly equal distribution on both surfaces is a feature of isobilateral (monocot) leaves, not dorsiventral leaves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A vertical section through the lamina of a dorsiventral leaf shows three main parts. Which of the following correctly names them?","image":"","options":["Epidermis, cortex and pith","Epidermis, mesophyll and vascular system","Hypodermis, mesophyll and pericycle","Cuticle, ground tissue and medullary rays"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says a vertical section of a dorsiventral leaf through the lamina shows three main parts: epidermis, mesophyll and vascular system.\nCortex, pith, pericycle and medullary rays are zones of stems and roots. They are not the parts of a leaf lamina.\nThe cuticle is only a waxy layer over the epidermis, so it is not a main part on its own.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Root hairs help in the absorption of water and minerals from the soil.\nReason (R): Root hairs are multicellular, branched outgrowths of the root epidermis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: root hairs absorb water and minerals from the soil.\nR is false: root hairs are unicellular elongations of epidermal cells and are unbranched.\nMulticellular, branched hairs describe shoot trichomes, not root hairs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the leaves of grasses, bulliform cells are best described as:","image":"","options":["Large, empty, colourless cells formed by modification of certain adaxial epidermal cells along the veins","Dumb-bell shaped guard cells present on the abaxial epidermis","Elongated, chloroplast-rich palisade cells arranged vertically below the upper epidermis","Thick-walled bundle sheath cells surrounding the vascular bundles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in grasses, some adaxial (upper) epidermal cells along the veins change into large, empty, colourless cells. These are the bulliform cells.\nOption (2) is wrong because dumb-bell shaped cells are the guard cells of grass stomata. They are not bulliform cells.\nOption (3) is wrong because palisade cells are mesophyll cells. They are also absent as a distinct layer in isobilateral leaves.\nOption (4) is wrong because bundle sheath cells surround the vascular bundles and are not epidermal.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the vertical section of a dicot leaf, the vascular bundles in the veins are surrounded by a layer of:","image":"","options":["Thin-walled chloroplast-rich palisade cells","Thick-walled bundle sheath cells","Collenchymatous hypodermis","Suberised endodermis with casparian strips"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the vascular bundles of a dorsiventral leaf are surrounded by a layer of thick-walled bundle sheath cells.\nCasparian strips belong to the endodermis of roots, not leaves.\nCollenchymatous hypodermis is found in dicot stems.\nPalisade cells are part of the mesophyll and do not enclose the bundles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Hypodermis of young dicot stem\n(B) Hypodermis of monocot stem\n(C) Ground tissue of monocot stem\n(D) Cavities within monocot stem vascular bundles\nList-II\n(I) Water\n(II) Parenchyma\n(III) Collenchyma\n(IV) Sclerenchyma","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: the dicot stem hypodermis is a few layers of collenchyma.\nB-IV: the monocot stem has a sclerenchymatous hypodermis.\nC-II: the monocot stem has a large, conspicuous parenchymatous ground tissue.\nD-I: the vascular bundles of the monocot stem contain water-containing cavities.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The innermost layer of the cortex of a dicot stem is also called the starch sheath. Why?","image":"","options":["Its cells carry casparian strips of starch","Its cells are rich in starch grains","It surrounds the starch-storing pith","It is made of sclerenchyma impregnated with starch"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The innermost cortical layer of the dicot stem is the endodermis.\nIts cells are rich in starch grains, so the layer is called the starch sheath.\nCasparian strips are made of suberin, not starch, and NCERT describes them for the endodermis of the dicot root, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the T.S. of a young dicot stem are correct?\n(i) The epidermis is covered by a thin cuticle and may bear trichomes and a few stomata.\n(ii) The cortical cells below the hypodermis are rounded, thin-walled parenchyma with conspicuous intercellular spaces.\n(iii) The pericycle forms a continuous ring of collenchyma.\n(iv) The central pith consists of rounded parenchyma cells with large intercellular spaces.\n(v) Phloem parenchyma is absent from the vascular bundles.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the NCERT description of the dicot stem epidermis.\n(ii) Correct: this layer is the general cortex.\n(iii) Incorrect: the pericycle forms semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches, not a continuous collenchyma ring.\n(iv) Correct.\n(v) Incorrect: absence of phloem parenchyma is a monocot stem feature.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Monocotyledonous roots do not undergo secondary growth.\nReason (R): Monocot roots usually have more than six xylem bundles.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Monocot roots show no secondary growth.\nR is true. Monocot roots are polyarch, with usually more than six xylem bundles.\nHowever, R does not explain A. Secondary growth depends on a vascular cambium forming. In dicot roots this cambium arises between xylem and phloem, and no such cambium develops in monocot roots. The number of xylem bundles is unrelated.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Guard cell\nB. Subsidiary cell\nC. Stomatal pore\nD. Trichome\nList-II\n(i) Aperture through which gaseous exchange and transpiration occur\n(ii) Usually multicellular epidermal hair of the shoot that helps reduce water loss\n(iii) Bean-shaped, chloroplast-containing cell enclosing the aperture\n(iv) Epidermal cell near the guard cell, specialised in shape and size","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): guard cells are bean-shaped, contain chloroplasts and enclose the pore.\nB-(iv): subsidiary cells are specialised epidermal cells next to the guard cells.\nC-(i): the stomatal pore is the opening for gaseous exchange and transpiration.\nD-(ii): shoot trichomes are usually multicellular and help prevent water loss.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the transverse section of a sunflower root, which is the outermost layer?","image":"","options":["Hypodermis","Epiblema","Endodermis","Cuticle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The outermost layer of the dicot (sunflower) root is the epiblema, and many of its cells grow out as unicellular root hairs.\nHypodermis is a sub-zone of the cortex in the dicot stem, not the root.\nEndodermis is the innermost layer of the cortex.\nCuticle is absent in roots.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a young plant organ shows the following:\n- a few layers of collenchyma just below the epidermis\n- an innermost cortical layer rich in starch grains\n- semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem\n- radially placed parenchyma cells between vascular bundles arranged in a ring\nWhich statement correctly describes the vascular bundles of this organ?","image":"","options":["Conjoint, open, with endarch protoxylem","Radial, with xylem and phloem on alternate radii","Conjoint, closed, each surrounded by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath","Conjoint, open, with exarch protoxylem and phloem on the inner side of xylem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Identify the organ. Collenchymatous hypodermis, a starch sheath, sclerenchymatous pericycle patches, medullary rays and a ring of bundles together indicate a dicot stem.\nStep 2: Recall its bundle type. Each bundle is conjoint and open (cambium present), with endarch protoxylem.\nWhy the others fail: radial bundles occur in roots. Closed, sheathed bundles occur in the monocot stem. In conjoint bundles, phloem lies on the outer side of xylem, not the inner side.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (part of dorsiventral leaf) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(a) Adaxial epidermis\n(b) Palisade parenchyma\n(c) Spongy parenchyma\n(d) Bundle sheath\nList-II\n(i) Extends from below palisade up to the lower epidermis\n(ii) Thick-walled cells around vascular bundles\n(iii) May even lack stomata\n(iv) Elongated cells arranged vertically and parallel","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Adaxial epidermis has fewer stomata and may even lack them, so (a) matches (iii).\n(b) Palisade parenchyma has elongated cells arranged vertically and parallel, so (b) matches (iv).\n(c) Spongy parenchyma lies below the palisade and extends to the lower epidermis, so (c) matches (i).\n(d) Bundle sheath is a layer of thick-walled cells around the vascular bundles, so (d) matches (ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following plant organs is the cuticle absent from the epidermis?","image":"","options":["Young stem","Leaf","Root","Petiole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The cuticle is a waxy layer that often covers the outside of the epidermis and prevents water loss.\nNCERT states that the cuticle is absent in roots.\nRoots must absorb water from the soil, so a waterproof coating would not suit them.\nThe young stem, leaf and petiole are aerial organs, and their epidermis carries a cuticle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the dorsiventral leaf are correct?\n(i) The mesophyll is made of chloroplast-containing parenchyma that carries out photosynthesis.\n(ii) Spongy parenchyma lies below the palisade cells and extends up to the lower epidermis.\n(iii) Within a vascular bundle, the xylem faces the abaxial side and the phloem faces the adaxial side.\n(iv) Vascular bundles can be seen in the veins as well as in the midrib.\n(v) The mesophyll is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the mesophyll is parenchyma with chloroplasts and is the site of photosynthesis.\n(ii) Correct: spongy parenchyma lies beneath the palisade and reaches the lower epidermis.\n(iii) Incorrect: in a leaf vascular bundle, the xylem lies towards the adaxial (upper) side and the phloem towards the abaxial (lower) side.\n(iv) Correct: vascular bundles occur in both the veins and the midrib.\n(v) Incorrect: an undifferentiated mesophyll is a feature of the isobilateral leaf. A dorsiventral leaf has distinct palisade and spongy layers.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a dorsiventral leaf, the vascular bundles differ in size because the veins vary in thickness in reticulate venation.\nStatement II: The mesophyll of a dorsiventral leaf is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Vascular bundle size depends on vein size, and in the reticulate venation of dicot leaves the veins vary in thickness.\nStatement II is incorrect. The dorsiventral leaf clearly shows both palisade and spongy parenchyma.\nAn undifferentiated mesophyll is a feature of the isobilateral (monocot) leaf.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the monocot root is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The pericycle, vascular bundles and pith lying inside the endodermis together constitute the stele.","Its epidermis is not covered by a cuticle.","Because it has a larger number of xylem bundles than the dicot root, it produces more secondary xylem.","Xylem and phloem are arranged alternately on different radii."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: all tissues inside the endodermis (pericycle, vascular bundles, pith) form the stele.\n(2) Correct: a cuticle is absent in roots.\n(3) Incorrect: polyarch xylem has nothing to do with secondary growth. Monocot roots do not undergo any secondary growth, so they form no secondary xylem at all.\n(4) Correct: roots have radial vascular bundles.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match the followings and choose the correct option from below\nColumn I: A. Meristem, B. Parenchyma, C. Collenchyma, D. Sclerenchyma, E. Epidermal tissue\nColumn II: i. Photosynthesis, storage, ii. mechanical support, iii. Actively dividing cells, iv. stomata, v. sclereids","image":"","options":["A-i, B-iii, C-v, D-ii, E-iv","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-v, E-iv","A-ii, B-iv, C-v, D-i, E-iii","A-v, B-iv, C-iii, D-ii, E-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Meristem: actively dividing cells, so A-iii.\nB. Parenchyma: photosynthesis and storage, so B-i.\nC. Collenchyma: living cells that give mechanical support, so C-ii.\nD. Sclerenchyma: includes sclereids (and fibres), so D-v.\nE. Epidermal tissue system: includes stomata, so E-iv.\nOnly option (2) has all five pairs correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The vascular bundles of monocotyledons are described as closed.\nReason (R): Monocot vascular bundles lack cambium and therefore do not form secondary tissues.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT calls monocot vascular bundles closed.\nR is true: no cambium is present in these bundles, so no secondary xylem or phloem is formed.\nR explains A, because a bundle is called closed precisely because it has no cambium and cannot form secondary tissues.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The vascular bundles of monocotyledons are described as 'closed' because they:","image":"","options":["are completely surrounded by a layer of endodermis","lack cambium and therefore cannot form secondary tissues","have phloem present on both sides of the xylem","lack phloem parenchyma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that monocot vascular bundles have no cambium. Without cambium they cannot form secondary xylem and phloem, so they are called closed.\nA bundle sheath around the bundle does not decide whether it is open or closed.\nThe absence of phloem parenchyma is a real feature of the monocot stem, but it is not the reason for the term 'closed'.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Conjoint vascular bundles are common in stems and leaves.\nStatement II: In radial vascular bundles, the xylem and phloem lie together on the same radius.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that conjoint bundles are common in stems and leaves.\nStatement II is incorrect: in radial bundles, xylem and phloem lie on different, alternating radii.\nLying together on the same radius is the defining feature of conjoint bundles, not radial ones.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features correctly describe the vascular bundles of a typical young dicot stem?\nConjoint; Open; Radial; Endarch protoxylem; Scattered in ground tissue; Arranged in a ring","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Conjoint: correct.\nOpen: correct, because cambium is present.\nRadial: incorrect, since radial bundles are found in roots.\nEndarch protoxylem: correct.\nScattered in ground tissue: incorrect, as this is a monocot stem feature.\nArranged in a ring: correct, and characteristic of the dicot stem.\nSo 4 features are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"When we peel the skin of a potato tuber, we remove","image":"","options":["Periderm","Epidermis","Cuticle","Sapwood"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A potato tuber is a modified underground stem.\nIts outer protective skin is periderm, not epidermis. Periderm is made up of phellem (cork), phellogen (cork cambium) and phelloderm.\nPeeling the skin therefore removes the periderm.\nEpidermis is the tempting wrong option, but in the mature tuber it is replaced by periderm. Cuticle and sapwood are not part of the potato skin.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q15)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the monocot stem is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The hypodermis is sclerenchymatous","Each vascular bundle is surrounded by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath","Peripheral vascular bundles are generally larger than the centrally located ones","Phloem parenchyma is absent and water-containing cavities occur in the vascular bundles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the monocot stem has a sclerenchymatous hypodermis.\n(2) Correct: its scattered bundles each have a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath.\n(3) Incorrect: peripheral bundles are generally smaller than the central ones, not larger.\n(4) Correct: phloem parenchyma is absent, and water-containing cavities are present in the bundles.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the vascular bundles of a dicotyledonous stem, which feature allows them to form secondary xylem and secondary phloem and so be called 'open'?","image":"","options":["Presence of a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath around the bundle","Presence of cambium between phloem and xylem","Arrangement of xylem and phloem on alternate radii","Presence of water-containing cavities within the bundle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: in dicot stems, cambium lies between the phloem and the xylem.\nBecause of this cambium, the bundle can form secondary xylem and phloem. Such bundles are therefore called open.\nA sclerenchymatous bundle sheath and water-containing cavities are features of monocot stem bundles, which are closed.\nXylem and phloem on alternate radii describes radial bundles, not open ones.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pericycle of a dicot root is a part of the ground tissue system even though it lies inside the endodermis.\nReason (R): Lateral roots and the vascular cambium originate from the pericycle cells.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Ground tissue covers all tissues except the epidermis and the vascular bundles. NCERT lists the pericycle as one zone of ground tissue, even though it lies within the stele.\nR is true. NCERT states that lateral roots and the vascular cambium (during secondary growth) are initiated in the pericycle.\nHowever, R does not explain A. The pericycle counts as ground tissue because of where it lies, not because of what develops from it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following and choose the correct option from below\nColumn I: A. Cuticle, B. Bulliform cells, C. Stomata, D. Epidermis\nColumn II: i. guard cells, ii. single layer, iii. waxy layer, iv. empty colourless cell","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Cuticle: the waxy layer covering the epidermis, so A-iii.\nB. Bulliform cells: large, empty, colourless cells in grass leaves, so B-iv.\nC. Stomata: each pore is bounded by two guard cells, so C-i.\nD. Epidermis: usually a single layer of cells, so D-ii.\nOption (1) matches every pair. Option (4) wrongly pairs B with 'single layer'.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Permanent tissues of plants are classified into simple and complex tissues.\nStatement II: Xylem and phloem are complex tissues, and together they constitute the vascular bundles.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT divides permanent tissues into simple and complex types.\nStatement II is correct. The vascular system consists of the complex tissues phloem and xylem, which together form vascular bundles.\nParenchyma, collenchyma and sclerenchyma are the simple tissues, and they make up the ground tissue.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Bulliform cells\nB. Casparian strips of suberin\nC. Semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma\nD. Collenchyma giving mechanical strength\nList-II\n(i) Pericycle of dicot stem\n(ii) Endodermis of dicot root\n(iii) Adaxial epidermis of grass leaf\n(iv) Hypodermis of young dicot stem","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Bulliform cells are modified adaxial epidermal cells along the veins of grass leaves.\nB-(ii): Casparian strips occur on the walls of root endodermis cells.\nC-(i): In the dicot stem, the pericycle forms semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches above the phloem.\nD-(iv): The collenchymatous hypodermis gives mechanical strength to the young dicot stem.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the transverse section of a young dicotyledonous stem, which layer is also called the starch sheath?","image":"","options":["Hypodermis","Pericycle","Endodermis","Medullary rays"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The endodermis is the innermost layer of the cortex in a dicot stem.\nIts cells are rich in starch grains, so this layer is also called the starch sheath.\nThe hypodermis is collenchymatous and gives mechanical strength. The pericycle forms sclerenchyma patches above the phloem. Neither is known for storing starch.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a dicot root, which layer gives rise to lateral roots?","image":"","options":["Endodermis","Cortex","Pericycle","Conjunctive tissue"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pericycle lies just inside the endodermis and is made of a few layers of thick-walled parenchymatous cells.\nLateral roots are initiated in the pericycle cells.\nThe vascular cambium also begins in these cells during secondary growth.\nThe endodermis is a single barrel-shaped layer with Casparian strips. It does not form lateral roots.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Interfascicular cambium and cork cambium are formed due to","image":"","options":["Cell division","Cell differentiation","Cell dedifferentiation","Redifferentiation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Interfascicular cambium arises from medullary ray cells. Cork cambium (phellogen) usually arises from cortical cells.\nThese are permanent cells that regain the power to divide. This process is called dedifferentiation, and it produces secondary meristems.\nRedifferentiation is the reverse process: cells produced by such meristems lose the ability to divide again and mature.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the T.S. of a dicot stem, how does the pericycle appear?","image":"","options":["As a continuous ring of collenchyma below the epidermis","As a single layer of barrel-shaped cells with casparian strips","As semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem","As radially placed parenchyma cells between the vascular bundles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The pericycle of the dicot stem lies on the inner side of the endodermis and above the phloem.\nIt appears as semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma.\nOption (1) describes the hypodermis, option (2) the root endodermis and option (4) the medullary rays.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following tissues is NOT seen in the transverse section of a mature monocot root?","image":"","options":["Endodermis","Pericycle","Vascular cambium","Pith"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A monocot root has epidermis, cortex, endodermis, pericycle, vascular bundles and pith, just like a dicot root.\nIt never develops a vascular cambium, which is why it shows no secondary growth.\nEndodermis, pericycle and pith are all present, so they cannot be the answer.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Identify the simple tissue system from the following","image":"","options":["Parenchyma","Xylem","Epidermis","Phloem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A simple tissue is made of only one type of cell. Parenchyma consists only of parenchyma cells, so it is simple. Collenchyma and sclerenchyma are the other simple tissues.\nXylem (tracheids, vessels, fibres, parenchyma) and phloem (sieve tubes, companion cells, fibres, parenchyma) are complex tissues.\nEpidermis is part of the epidermal tissue system and contains several cell types: epidermal cells, guard cells and trichomes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Root hairs are unicellular elongations of epidermal cells that help absorb water and minerals.\nStatement II: Trichomes on the shoot system are usually multicellular and may even be secretory.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Root hairs are single-celled outgrowths of the epidermal cells, and they absorb water and minerals from the soil.\nStatement II is correct. Shoot trichomes are usually multicellular. They may be branched or unbranched, soft or stiff, and some are secretory.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The vascular bundles of a primary dicot root are of the radial type and not conjoint.\nReason (R): In roots, xylem and phloem patches are arranged alternately on different radii, rather than together on the same radius.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT describes the arrangement in roots as radial, with two to four separate xylem and phloem patches in a dicot root.\nR is true. A radial arrangement means xylem and phloem alternate along different radii.\nIn conjoint bundles, by contrast, xylem and phloem lie together on the same radius, as in stems and leaves.\nSo R correctly explains why root bundles are radial and not conjoint.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines the T.S. of a young sunflower stem. Starting from the outer surface, she moves inward along a radius that passes through a vascular bundle, until she reaches the centre. Which sequence of tissues will she encounter?","image":"","options":["Epidermis → hypodermis → cortical parenchyma → endodermis → pericycle → phloem → cambium → protoxylem → metaxylem → pith","Epidermis → hypodermis → cortical parenchyma → endodermis → pericycle → phloem → cambium → metaxylem → protoxylem → pith","Epidermis → cortical parenchyma → hypodermis → pericycle → endodermis → phloem → cambium → metaxylem → protoxylem → pith","Epidermis → hypodermis → cortical parenchyma → endodermis → pericycle → xylem → cambium → phloem → pith"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Outer region: the cortex lies between the epidermis and the pericycle. Its order is hypodermis, then cortical parenchyma, then endodermis.\nNext comes the pericycle, which lies inside the endodermis and above the phloem.\nThe bundle is conjoint, with phloem on the outer side and cambium between phloem and xylem.\nThe protoxylem is endarch, so it faces the pith. Going inward, metaxylem therefore comes before protoxylem, and then the pith.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes a typical epidermal cell?","image":"","options":["Sclerenchymatous, dead, with lignified walls","Collenchymatous, with thickened corners","Parenchymatous, with a small amount of cytoplasm lining the cell wall and a large vacuole","Parenchymatous, with dense cytoplasm and no vacuole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes epidermal cells as parenchymatous.\nThey have a small amount of cytoplasm lining the cell wall and a large central vacuole.\nThey are living cells, so the 'dead, lignified' option is wrong.\nCorner thickenings are a feature of collenchyma.\nDense cytoplasm with no vacuole is typical of meristematic cells, not epidermal cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"An epidermal peel of a leaf shows stomata with dumb-bell shaped guard cells. Which other feature is most likely to be seen in a vertical section of the same leaf?","image":"","options":["Elongated palisade cells beneath the upper epidermis only, with spongy parenchyma below","Stomata on both surfaces and mesophyll not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma","Vascular bundles of widely varying sizes because of reticulate venation","Stomata present only on the abaxial surface, with none on the adaxial surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Dumb-bell shaped guard cells are characteristic of grasses, so this is a monocot leaf.\nStep 2: A monocot (isobilateral) leaf has stomata on both surfaces, and its mesophyll is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.\nOptions (1), (3) and (4) are features of a dorsiventral dicot leaf. These are differentiated mesophyll, reticulate venation with varied bundle sizes, and stomata mostly or only on the abaxial surface.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure) with List-II (where it is found):\nList-I\n(a) Collenchymatous hypodermis\n(b) Sclerenchymatous hypodermis\n(c) Casparian strips\n(d) Bulliform cells\nList-II\n(i) Endodermis of root\n(ii) Adaxial epidermis of grass leaf\n(iii) Dicot stem\n(iv) Monocot stem","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The dicot stem has a hypodermis of a few layers of collenchyma, so a matches iii.\n(b) The monocot stem has a sclerenchymatous hypodermis, so b matches iv.\n(c) Casparian strips are suberin deposits on the walls of root endodermis cells, so c matches i.\n(d) Bulliform cells are modified adaxial epidermal cells along the veins of grass leaves, so d matches ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Monocots and dicots differ markedly in their internal structure. Which of the following statements about these differences is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A monocot root usually has more than six xylem bundles, whereas a dicot root usually has two to four","The pith is large and well developed in a monocot root but small or inconspicuous in a dicot root","In an isobilateral (monocot) leaf, the mesophyll is differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma","Vascular bundles of a monocot stem are conjoint, closed and surrounded by a sclerenchymatous bundle sheath"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: monocot roots are polyarch (more than six xylem bundles), while dicot roots have two to four xylem patches.\n(2) Correct: the pith is large in a monocot root and small or inconspicuous in a dicot root.\n(3) Incorrect: in an isobilateral leaf the mesophyll is NOT differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma; that differentiation is found in the dorsiventral (dicot) leaf.\n(4) Correct: monocot stem bundles are conjoint, closed and sheathed by sclerenchyma.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about vascular tissues is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Monocot stem bundles are conjoint and closed, so they produce no secondary xylem or phloem","Since roots have radial vascular bundles, cambium can never develop in any root","In a young dicot root, a cambium ring later develops between the xylem and phloem patches","Presence or absence of cambium is the basis for calling a bundle open or closed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: monocot stem bundles are conjoint and closed, so no secondary tissues form.\n(2) Incorrect: radial bundles do not prevent cambium formation. NCERT notes that a cambium ring later develops between the xylem and phloem in dicot roots.\n(3) Correct, as stated in NCERT.\n(4) Correct: cambium present means open; cambium absent means closed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines the T.S. of a young stem. She notes a sclerenchymatous layer below the epidermis and numerous vascular bundles scattered in a large parenchymatous tissue. She also sees that the bundles near the outside are smaller than those near the centre, and she identifies the stem as a monocot. Which additional observation would CONTRADICT her identification?","image":"","options":["Water-containing cavities present inside the vascular bundles","A strip of cambium present between the xylem and phloem of each bundle","A sheath of sclerenchyma surrounding each vascular bundle","No phloem parenchyma visible in the phloem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The features she has already noted all fit a monocot stem.\nOptions (1), (3) and (4) are also NCERT features of monocot stem bundles: water cavities, a sclerenchymatous sheath and absence of phloem parenchyma.\nCambium between xylem and phloem makes a bundle open, which is a dicot stem feature. Monocot stem bundles are closed.\nSo option (2) contradicts the identification.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Stomata regulate transpiration and gaseous exchange.\n(b) Each stoma consists of two guard cells enclosing a stomatal pore.\n(c) Guard cells lack chloroplasts.\n(d) The inner walls of guard cells are thin and the outer walls are thick.\n(e) In a dorsiventral leaf, the adaxial epidermis generally bears more stomata than the abaxial epidermis.\n(f) Subsidiary cells form part of the stomatal apparatus.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: stomata regulate transpiration and gaseous exchange.\n(b) Correct: two guard cells enclose the pore.\n(c) Incorrect: guard cells possess chloroplasts.\n(d) Incorrect: the inner walls are thick and the outer walls are thin.\n(e) Incorrect: the abaxial (lower) epidermis generally has more stomata, and the adaxial surface may even lack them.\n(f) Correct: the stomatal apparatus is the pore plus guard cells plus subsidiary cells.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Epiblema of roots is equivalent to","image":"","options":["Pericycle","Endodermis","Epidermis","Stele"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The outermost layer of the root is called the epiblema or rhizodermis. It is the root's epidermis.\nMany epiblema cells grow out as unicellular root hairs.\nPericycle and endodermis are inner layers lying around or inside the stele. The stele is the central vascular region.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which wall of a guard cell is highly thickened?","image":"","options":["The outer wall, facing away from the stomatal pore","The inner wall, facing the stomatal pore","Both inner and outer walls equally","Only the wall in contact with the subsidiary cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the outer walls of guard cells, away from the pore, are thin.\nThe inner walls, towards the stomatal pore, are highly thickened.\nOption (1) reverses this fact and is the commonest error.\nThickening is not uniform, which rules out option (3). It is not linked to the subsidiary cells either, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about an isobilateral leaf are correct?\n(i) Stomata occur on both epidermal surfaces.\n(ii) Palisade parenchyma lies adaxially and spongy parenchyma lies abaxially.\n(iii) In grasses, bulliform cells occur in the abaxial epidermis.\n(iv) Vascular bundles, except those of main veins, are of nearly similar size.\n(v) Bulliform cells are large, empty and colourless.","image":"","options":["2","4","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: stomata are present on both surfaces.\n(ii) Incorrect: the mesophyll is not differentiated into palisade and spongy parenchyma.\n(iii) Incorrect: bulliform cells come from adaxial (upper) epidermal cells.\n(iv) Correct: this reflects parallel venation.\n(v) Correct: this is how NCERT describes bulliform cells.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A conjoint and open vascular bundle will be observed in the transverse section of","image":"","options":["Monocot root","Monocot stem","Dicot root","Dicot stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Conjoint means xylem and phloem lie on the same radius. Open means cambium is present between them.\nIn a dicot stem, the bundles are conjoint, collateral and open, arranged in a ring.\nMonocot stem bundles are conjoint but closed, because they have no cambium.\nRoots, both dicot and monocot, have radial bundles, not conjoint ones.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a root shows more than six xylem bundles, a large, well-developed pith, and no sign of secondary growth. The section is most likely of a:","image":"","options":["Dicot root in its primary state","Monocot root","Dicot stem","Monocot stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"More than six xylem bundles (polyarch) and a large, well-developed pith are features of the monocot root.\nMonocot roots do not undergo secondary growth.\nA primary dicot root has only two to four xylem patches and a small or inconspicuous pith, so option (1) does not fit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Epidermal tissue system\n(b) Ground tissue system of stem and root\n(c) Vascular tissue system\n(d) Ground tissue of leaf\nList-II\n(i) Mesophyll\n(ii) Xylem and phloem\n(iii) Stomata and trichomes\n(iv) Cortex, pericycle and pith","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Epidermal: made of epidermal cells, stomata and appendages such as trichomes, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Ground: in primary stems and roots it is divided into cortex, pericycle and pith, so it matches (iv).\n(c) Vascular: formed by the complex tissues xylem and phloem, so it matches (ii).\n(d) In leaves, the ground tissue consists of chloroplast-containing thin-walled cells called mesophyll, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The pith of a monocot root is large and well developed, whereas that of a dicot root is small or inconspicuous.\nStatement II: The pith of a dicot stem consists of compactly arranged cells without intercellular spaces.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes the dicot root pith as small or inconspicuous and the monocot root pith as large and well developed.\nStatement II is incorrect. The dicot stem pith is made of many rounded parenchymatous cells with large intercellular spaces, in the central part of the stem.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The spongy parenchyma of a dorsiventral leaf has numerous large spaces and air cavities.\nReason (R): Spongy parenchyma cells are oval or round and loosely arranged.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes numerous large spaces and air cavities between spongy parenchyma cells.\nR is true. These cells are oval or round and loosely arranged.\nBecause rounded cells are packed loosely, gaps remain between them. These gaps form the large spaces and air cavities.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the epidermal tissue system are correct?\n(i) The epidermis is usually multilayered.\n(ii) Epidermal cells are elongated, compactly arranged and parenchymatous.\n(iii) The cuticle is a waxy layer that covers the epidermis of roots as well as shoots.\n(iv) Shoot trichomes may be branched or unbranched, and soft or stiff.\n(v) Some trichomes may be secretory.\n(vi) Root hairs absorb water and minerals from the soil.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the epidermis is usually single-layered.\n(ii) Correct: epidermal cells are elongated, compact and parenchymatous, with a large vacuole.\n(iii) Incorrect: the cuticle is absent in roots.\n(iv) Correct: shoot trichomes may be branched or unbranched, and soft or stiff.\n(v) Correct: some trichomes are secretory.\n(vi) Correct: root hairs absorb water and minerals from the soil.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The waxy layer that covers the outer surface of the epidermis and reduces water loss is absent in which of the following plant parts?","image":"","options":["Leaf lamina","Young aerial stem","Root","Leaf petiole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The cuticle is a thick, waxy layer on the outside of the epidermis that prevents water loss.\nNCERT states that the cuticle is absent in roots.\nRoots absorb water from the soil, so a water-impermeable covering on their epidermis would block absorption.\nLeaves, young stems and petioles are aerial parts exposed to the air, so they carry a cuticle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical conjoint vascular bundle of a stem, where is the phloem usually located with respect to the xylem?","image":"","options":["On the inner side of the xylem","Only on the outer side of the xylem","On an alternate radius from the xylem","Completely surrounding the xylem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: conjoint vascular bundles usually have phloem located only on the outer side of the xylem.\nAn alternate radius describes radial bundles, which occur in roots, not conjoint bundles.\nPhloem on the inner side, or phloem completely surrounding the xylem, is not the usual condition described for conjoint bundles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"When the xylem and phloem in a plant organ lie in separate patches on different, alternating radii, the arrangement is called radial. In which organ is this arrangement typically seen?","image":"","options":["Dicot stem","Monocot stem","Root","Leaf midrib"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the radial arrangement as xylem and phloem placed alternately along different radii, as seen in roots.\nStems and leaves usually have conjoint bundles. In a conjoint bundle, xylem and phloem lie together on the same radius.\nSo dicot stem, monocot stem and the leaf midrib are all wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In grasses, the guard cells of stomata are:","image":"","options":["Bean-shaped","Dumb-bell shaped","Kidney-shaped with thin inner walls","Barrel-shaped"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: each stoma is formed by two guard cells around a stomatal pore.\nIn most plants, such as dicots, the guard cells are bean-shaped.\nIn grasses, the guard cells are dumb-bell shaped.\nBarrel-shaped describes endodermal cells of the root, not guard cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (region of dicot stem) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(A) Hypodermis\n(B) Pericycle\n(C) Medullary rays\n(D) Pith\nList-II\n(i) Radially placed parenchyma cells between vascular bundles\n(ii) Rounded parenchyma cells with large intercellular spaces in the centre\n(iii) Collenchyma giving mechanical strength to the young stem\n(iv) Semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches above phloem","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Hypodermis: a few layers of collenchyma that give mechanical strength, so (iii).\n(B) Pericycle: semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem, so (iv).\n(C) Medullary rays: a few layers of radially placed parenchyma between the vascular bundles, so (i).\n(D) Pith: rounded parenchyma cells with large intercellular spaces in the central portion, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (region of dicot root) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\n(A) Epiblema\n(B) Cortex\n(C) Endodermis\n(D) Pericycle\nList-II:\n(i) Barrel-shaped cells with suberised Casparian strips\n(ii) Few layers of thick-walled parenchyma; initiates lateral roots\n(iii) Unicellular root hairs\n(iv) Several layers of thin-walled parenchyma with intercellular spaces","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): the epiblema bears unicellular root hairs.\nB-(iv): the cortex is made of many layers of thin-walled parenchyma with intercellular spaces.\nC-(i): the endodermis has barrel-shaped cells with Casparian strips of suberin.\nD-(ii): the pericycle is a few layers of thick-walled parenchyma and gives rise to lateral roots.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the transverse section of a root, xylem and phloem lie on different radii, alternating with each other around the centre. What is this arrangement of vascular tissue called?","image":"","options":["Conjoint open","Conjoint closed","Radial","Scattered"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In roots, xylem and phloem patches are not on the same radius. They alternate along different radii, and NCERT calls this a radial arrangement.\nConjoint bundles (open or closed) have xylem and phloem together on the same radius, as in stems and leaves.\n'Scattered' describes how bundles are distributed in a monocot stem, not how xylem and phloem are placed within a bundle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Vascular bundles of monocotyledons are described as closed.\nReason (R): Monocot vascular bundles have no cambium, so they cannot form secondary xylem and phloem.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT calls monocot vascular bundles closed.\nR is true: these bundles lack cambium, so no secondary tissues are formed.\nThe absence of cambium is the very reason the bundles are called closed. Dicot stem bundles, which have cambium, are called open.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of stem is stained first with safranin and then with fast green following the usual schedule of double staining for the preparation of a permanent slide. What would be the colour of the stained xylem and phloem?","image":"","options":["Red and green","Green and red","Orange and yellow","Purple and orange"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In double staining, safranin binds strongly to lignin, so lignified walls stain red.\nFast green stains non-lignified, cellulose-rich walls green.\nXylem (tracheids, vessels) has lignified walls, so it appears red. Phloem (sieve tubes, companion cells) has cellulose walls, so it appears green.\nOption (2) reverses the colours. Options (3) and (4) are not the colours these two stains give.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about trichomes is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Trichomes of the shoot system are usually multicellular","Trichomes may be branched or unbranched","Trichomes are always unicellular and can never be secretory","Trichomes help in preventing water loss due to transpiration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: shoot trichomes are usually multicellular.\n(2) Correct: trichomes may be branched or unbranched, and soft or stiff.\n(3) Incorrect: trichomes are usually multicellular and may even be secretory. Being unicellular is a feature of root hairs.\n(4) Correct: trichomes help reduce water loss through transpiration.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A piece of wood having no vessels (trachea) must belong to","image":"","options":["Teak","Mango","Pine","Palm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Vessels (tracheae) are characteristic of angiosperm xylem.\nGymnosperms generally lack vessels. Their xylem conducts water mainly through tracheids.\nPine is a gymnosperm, so its wood has no vessels and is called non-porous softwood.\nTeak and mango are dicot angiosperms, and palm is a monocot angiosperm. All three have vessels.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 6 (MCQ Q12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The vascular bundles of a dicot stem can produce secondary xylem and secondary phloem.\nReason (R): In a dicot stem, cambium is present between the phloem and the xylem of each vascular bundle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: dicot stem bundles form secondary tissues.\nR is true: cambium lies between the phloem and xylem, which is why these bundles are called open.\nIt is the presence of this cambium that gives the bundles the ability to form secondary xylem and phloem, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which set of characteristics correctly describes bulliform cells?","image":"","options":["Small, densely cytoplasmic, rich in chloroplasts","Thick-walled, lignified and dead","Dumb-bell shaped cells enclosing a pore","Large, empty and colourless"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT describes bulliform cells as large, empty, colourless cells formed from adaxial epidermal cells along veins in grasses.\nThey lack chloroplasts, so option (1) is wrong.\nDumb-bell shaped cells are the guard cells of grasses, not bulliform cells, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the primary structure of a dicot root is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The pith is small or inconspicuous.","The pericycle occurs as semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem.","The cortex consists of thin-walled parenchyma cells with intercellular spaces.","A cuticle is absent on the outer surface."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the pith of the dicot root is small or inconspicuous.\n(2) Incorrect: in the dicot root, the pericycle is a few layers of thick-walled parenchymatous cells. Semi-lunar sclerenchyma patches above the phloem describe the pericycle of the dicot stem.\n(3) Correct: the cortex has several layers of thin-walled parenchyma with intercellular spaces.\n(4) Correct: NCERT states that the cuticle is absent in roots.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about monocotyledonous roots is correct?","image":"","options":["They undergo extensive secondary growth.","They undergo secondary growth only in the pith region.","They do not undergo any secondary growth.","They undergo secondary growth only after lateral roots are formed."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT clearly states that monocotyledonous roots do not undergo any secondary growth.\nIn contrast, a cambium ring develops later in the dicot root between the xylem and phloem.\nThe other options all wrongly suggest that some secondary growth occurs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the stomatal apparatus is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Guard cells contain chloroplasts and regulate the opening and closing of stomata","The walls of guard cells facing the stomatal pore are highly thickened","The outer walls of guard cells, away from the pore, are thicker than the inner walls","Subsidiary cells are specialised epidermal cells and form part of the stomatal apparatus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: guard cells have chloroplasts and control the opening of stomata.\n(2) Correct: the inner walls, facing the pore, are highly thickened.\n(3) Incorrect: the outer walls, away from the pore, are thin, not thick.\n(4) Correct: the stomatal apparatus is made up of the pore, the guard cells and the subsidiary cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The study of the internal structure of plants is called","image":"","options":["Morphology","Anatomy","Physiology","Taxonomy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines anatomy as the study of the internal structure of plants.\nMorphology is the most tempting wrong option, but it deals with external form, not internal organisation.\nPhysiology studies functions, and taxonomy deals with classification.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the distribution of stomata in a typical dorsiventral (dicot) leaf?","image":"","options":["The adaxial epidermis bears more stomata than the abaxial epidermis","The abaxial epidermis generally bears more stomata, and the adaxial epidermis may even lack stomata","Stomata are distributed equally on both epidermal surfaces","Both epidermal surfaces lack stomata, and gases diffuse through the cuticle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a dorsiventral leaf, the abaxial (lower) epidermis generally carries more stomata than the adaxial (upper) epidermis.\nThe adaxial epidermis may have no stomata at all.\nEqual distribution on both surfaces is a feature of the isobilateral (monocot) leaf, so option (3) is wrong.\nThe cuticle is waxy and reduces water loss. It is not a route for gas exchange, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (ground tissue region) with List-II (type of cells forming it):\nList-I\nA. Hypodermis of dicot stem\nB. Hypodermis of monocot stem\nC. Pericycle of dicot root\nD. Palisade tissue of dorsiventral leaf\nList-II\n(i) Sclerenchyma\n(ii) Thick-walled parenchyma\n(iii) Collenchyma\n(iv) Elongated chloroplast-containing parenchyma","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(iii): The dicot stem hypodermis is a few layers of collenchyma.\nB-(i): The monocot stem has a sclerenchymatous hypodermis.\nC-(ii): The dicot root pericycle is a few layers of thick-walled parenchymatous cells.\nD-(iv): Palisade cells are elongated, chloroplast-containing parenchyma arranged vertically and parallel to each other.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Xylem bundles in dicot root\n(b) Xylem bundles in monocot root\n(c) Pith of dicot root\n(d) Pith of monocot root\nList-II\n(i) Large and well developed\n(ii) Usually two to four\n(iii) Small or inconspicuous\n(iv) Usually more than six","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Dicot root: usually two to four xylem and phloem patches, so (ii).\n(b) Monocot root: usually more than six xylem bundles (polyarch), so (iv).\n(c) Dicot root: pith small or inconspicuous, so (iii).\n(d) Monocot root: pith large and well developed, so (i).\nOption (3) swaps the two pith descriptions.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-186","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":186,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a dicot stem, a few layers of radially placed parenchymatous cells lie between adjacent vascular bundles. What are they called?","image":"","options":["Conjunctive tissue","Medullary rays","Bundle sheath","Pericycle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The radially arranged parenchyma between the vascular bundles of a dicot stem forms the medullary rays.\nConjunctive tissue is the parenchyma between the xylem and phloem patches of a root, not of a stem.\nA bundle sheath surrounds each bundle, as in monocot stems and leaves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-187","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":187,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The vascular bundles of a monocot stem do not give rise to secondary xylem and secondary phloem.\nReason (R): Cambium is absent between the xylem and phloem in the vascular bundles of a monocot stem.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: monocot stem bundles do not form secondary tissues.\nR is true: there is no cambium between their xylem and phloem.\nCambium is the tissue that produces secondary xylem and phloem. Its absence is exactly why the bundles are called closed and cannot form secondary tissues.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-188","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":188,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The vascular bundles of a dicotyledonous stem are described as open.\nReason (R): Cambium is present between the xylem and phloem of these bundles, enabling them to form secondary xylem and phloem.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: each vascular bundle of a dicot stem is conjoint and open.\nR is true: cambium lies between the phloem and xylem, and it can produce secondary tissues.\nR explains A, because a bundle is called 'open' precisely because it has cambium. Monocot bundles lack cambium and are therefore called closed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-ANATOMY-189","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","qno":189,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a young stem shows:\n- a few layers of collenchyma just below the epidermis\n- semi-lunar patches of sclerenchyma above the phloem\n- conjoint, open vascular bundles arranged in a ring, with endarch protoxylem\n\nWhich of the following would you NOT expect to see in this same section?","image":"","options":["Radially placed parenchyma cells between the vascular bundles forming medullary rays","An innermost cortical layer whose cells are rich in starch grains","Water-containing cavities within the vascular bundles","A central pith of rounded parenchyma cells with large intercellular spaces"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"These features identify the section as a dicot stem: collenchymatous hypodermis, sclerenchymatous pericycle patches, and open bundles in a ring.\nA dicot stem has medullary rays, a starch sheath (endodermis) and a large parenchymatous pith, so options 1, 2 and 4 are expected.\nWater-containing cavities within vascular bundles are a feature of the monocot stem, so they would not be seen here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 6 · Anatomy of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"}]});
